\subsection{Preliminaries}
Let $\Om$ be a bounded pseudoconvex domain in $\bbC ^{n}$ %. %with boundary $\partial \Om$. $\Om$ is smooth if there exists
and $\rho$ be a smooth defining function satisfying $\Om = \{z: \rho < 0 \}$ and $d\rho \neq 0$ on the boundary $\partial \Om$. The following result due to Kohn \cite{Kohn73} gives the explicit exhaustion of any bounded pseudoconvex domain by strongly pseudoconvex domains
(cf. Lemma 2.5 in \cite{S10}).

\begin{prop}\label{KohnA}
  Let $\Om$ be a bounded pseudoconvex domain in $\bbC ^{n}$ with a smooth defining function $\tilde\rho$. Let $\rho= e ^{- M |z| ^{2}} \tilde\rho \(z\) $. Then
   there is $M > 0$ such that the subdomains $\Om _{\delta}$ defined by $\Om _{\delta}: = \left\{z \in \Om: \rho_\delta(z):=\rho(z)+\delta<0 \right\}$ are strictly pseudoconvex subdomains of $\Om$
 for all $\delta > 0$ close enough to 0. %. Equivalently, the defining function for $\Om$, $r: = e ^{- M |z| ^{2}} \rho \(z\)$ has strictly level sets $\left\{r = - \delta\right\}$ for $\delta > 0$ close enough to 0.
\end{prop}

For each $0\leq \alpha \leq N$, let $U_\alpha$ be an open set in $\mathbb{C}^n$ such that $\lbk U_{\alpha} \rbk_{\alpha = 0} ^{N}$ is an open covering of $\overline{\Om}$ with $U_{0} \subset \subset  \Om$. Let $\{\chi_\alpha\}_{\alpha = 0} ^{N}$ be a family of smooth cut-off functions subordinate to $\lbk U_{\alpha} \rbk_{\alpha = 0} ^{N}$ such that each $\chi_\alpha$ is compactly supported in $U_\alpha$ with $\chi_\alpha$ is identically equal to 1 on $V_\alpha \subset\subset U_\alpha$ and $\sum_{\alpha=0}^N \chi_\alpha \equiv 1$ on $\overline{\Om}$. For any $U_{\alpha}$ with $1 \leq \alpha \leq N$, define vector fields $L _{1}, \cdots, L _{n - 1}$ of type $\(1, 0\)$ in $U _{\alpha}$ which are orthonormal with respect to the Euclidean norm and span the holomorphic tangent space $T^{(1, 0)} _{z}  \partial \Om _{\delta}$ for any $z \in U_j$, %where $\Om _{\varepsilon} = \{z \in \Om| \rho\(z\) < - \varepsilon \}$ 
with $\delta$ sufficiently close to 0. Define $L _{n} = \sum _{k = 1} ^{n} \frac{1}{|\partial \rho|^{2}} \frac{\partial \rho}{\partial\bar{z} _{k}} \frac{\partial}{\partial z _{k}}$ to be the $\(1, 0\)$ complex normal vector with unit Euclidean norm. It follows that $\{L _{1}, \cdots, L _{n - 1}, L _{n}\}$ forms an orthogonal basis of $T^{(1, 0)} _{z} \mathbb{C}^n$ for any $z\in U _{\alpha}$. Let $\omega _{1}, \omega _{2}, \cdots, \omega _{n}$ be the $\(0, 1\)$-forms dual to $L _{1}, L _{2}, \cdots, L _{n - 1}, L _{n}$ satisfying $\omega _{j} \(L _{k}\) = \delta _{jk}$. Then $\lbk \omega  _{j}\rbk _{j = 1} ^{n}$ forms an orthogonal basis of $\Lambda^{\(1, 0\)}_{z} \mathbb{C}^n$ for any $z\in U _{\alpha}$. %which are called a special boundary frame. 
These moving frames are referred to the special boundary chart as in \cite{FK1972}.

For a $(0, q)$-form $u$, write $u= \sum' _{|J|=q} u _{J} \bar{\omega} _{J} = \sum' _{|K|=q} u _{\tilde K} d \bar{z} _{K}$ with respect to the special boundary chart and Euclidean coordinates respectively.
Let $\phi$ be a smooth function on $\overline{\Om}$. Using special boundary charts, write $$\partial\bar\partial \phi =\sum _{i,j = 1} ^{n} \phi _{ij}\omega_i \wedge \bar\omega_j.$$ 
For any $\(0, q\)$-form $u$ on $\overline{\Om}$, write  $u =\sum _{\alpha = 0} ^{N} \chi _{\alpha} u$ with $\chi _{\alpha} u = \sum' _{|J|=q} u _{J} \bar{w} _{J}$ compactly supported in $U _{\alpha}$ for $1\leq \alpha \leq N$ and $\chi_{0} u$ compactly supported in $U_{0}$. 
When $\(0, q\)$-forms $u = \sum' _{J} u _{J} \bar{\omega} _{J}, v = \sum' _{I} v _{I} \bar{\omega} _{I}$ are supported in $U _{\alpha}$, the $L^2$ inner product is defined as 
\begin{align*}
  \(u, v\) 
  = \sideset{}{'}{\sum} _{J}\(u _{J} \bar{\omega} _{J}, v _{J} \bar{\omega} _{J}\) 
  = \sideset{}{'}{\sum} _{J} \int _{U _{\alpha}} u _{J} \bar{v} _{J} dV.
\end{align*}
