\begin{rmk}\label{increasing}
By Schur’s majorization theorem, the sum of the smallest $q$ eigenvalues of an $n \times n$ Hermitian matrix is less than or equal to the sum of the smallest $q$ diagonal entries of the same matrix. If $\Om$ possesses a family of transverse vector fields satisfying Property ($\widetilde{P} _{q} ^{\#}$), then $\mathcal{X}_{q} \(\lambda_\varepsilon\) \geq 0$ implies that $q \geq  s$ and the $(q+1)$-th smallest eigenvalue must be non-negative and thus $\mathcal{X}_{q+1} \(\lambda_\varepsilon\) \geq \mathcal{X}_{q} \(\lambda_\varepsilon\)$ and $\mathcal{Y}^{q+1} \(\lambda_\varepsilon\) \geq \mathcal{Y}^{q} \(\lambda_\varepsilon\). $ Since $\partial\bar\partial \rho_\delta$ is non-negative restricted to $T^{(1, 0)}\partial \Om_\delta$, (\ref{boundary estimate''}) holds for every $u \in  C _{\(0, q+1\)}^ \infty \(\cl{\Om_\delta} \) \cap \dom\(\bar{\partial}^*\)$ by the same reason. Therefore,  $\Om$ possesses a family of transverse vector fields satisfying Property ($\widetilde{P} _{q+1} ^{\#}$).
\end{rmk}

As in the proof of \autoref{boundary term}, (\ref{boundary estimate''}) holds automatically if $s=n-2, q=n-1$. This inspires us to define a weaker condition of $\widetilde{P}^{\#}_{n-1}$.

