\begin{abstract}
The notched stick, also known as the Gee-Haw-Whammy-Diddle, is a wooden toy able to convert linear vibration into rotational motion, whose behavior has been intriguing both children and physicists for decades. The oldest scientific article one can find on this subject was published 87 years ago in the present journal. Here we derive an analytical model of the system, supported by experimental results. We predict the direction of rotation, and explain why the device is so easy to operate, even without fine control of the various parameters. %We find that the effect is caused by an anisotropic restoring force acting on the notched stick, and that the elliptical trajectory of the stick is greatly influenced by the amount of mechanical energy loss. 
The potential importance of the vertical displacement of the finger exerting the perturbation force is also highlighted. %, an effect hitherto ignored. 
 %These results represent a major step towards a complete understanding of the device's physical mechanism. 
 We finally discuss similarities between the mechanical system described here and the optical effect of birefringence.   %The direction of rotation of the propeller at the end of the stick can be controlled by the players with proper positioning of the hand exciting

