Energy consumption in generative AI and large language models (LLMs) has become one of the urgent issues in deep learning. OpenAI reported that training the largest GPT-3 model \blue{consumed} approximately 1,300 MWh of electricity, which \blue{was} equivalent to the annual energy usage of about 130 U.S. homes \cite{OpenAIFewShot2020}. While there aren't many papers addressing the goal of low energy consumption in the context of LLMs, several reports indicate that some commercial LLMs have implemented this technology. This paper also aims to clarify the advancements in this area. Although \blue{our} research takes a different direction, there are studies that apply LLMs to the human activity recognition datasets we focus on. For example, recent studies~\cite{LLMHAR} and \cite{LLMHAR2} have explored the use of large language models (LLMs) for human activity recognition.


%現在, LLMに用いられて探求されている個々の計算負荷を減らす技術とは, 量子化, プルーニング, 知識蒸留などである. これらの技術を用いるとモデルのサイズや計算負荷を減らし, ハードウェアの最適化により, エネルギー消費を抑えて省電力化に寄与する. また, スパース化や早期終了といった効率的なアルゴリズム設計を用いることも, 計算資源を効果的に利用でき, 省電力化に寄与する. これらの技術により, モデルの性能を維持しつつ, 電力消費の削減を実現できる. さらに, マルチタスク学習や分散学習, エッジコンピューティングの活用も省電力化に寄与する. 動的モデルサイズや適応型推論を採用すると, 入力やタスクに応じた効率的な計算が可能になり, 不要な計算コストを削減する. ハイパーパラメータの最適化やモデルの再利用も, 学習時間やエネルギー消費を抑える. これらの工夫により, 特にエッジデバイスやモバイル環境でのディープラーニングのエネルギー効率が向上する. 

Currently, the technologies being explored in LLMs include quantization, pruning, and knowledge distillation. These methods help reduce model size and computational load, resulting in decreased energy consumption and power savings through hardware optimization. Additionally, the use of efficient algorithm designs, such as sparsification and early stopping, can effectively leverage computing resources and contribute to lower energy usage. These technologies can maintain model performance while reducing power consumption. Furthermore, multitask learning, distributed learning, and edge computing also play a role in energy savings. The adoption of dynamic model sizes and adaptive inference allows for efficient computation based on the input and task, minimizing unnecessary computational costs. Hyperparameter optimization and model reuse also contribute to reducing training time and energy consumption. These techniques enhance the energy efficiency of deep learning, particularly on edge devices and in mobile environments.

