\section{Introduction}
\label{Intro}


The term "\textit{personality}" derives from the Latin word "\textit{persona}," which is used in ancient Roman theater to represent the mask worn by actors. This mask symbolized a consistent pattern of behavior, which is the essence of the psychological term "\textit{personality}"—a dynamic organization of psychophysical systems that creates characteristic patterns of human behavior, thoughts, and feelings \cite{allport1936trait}. Understanding personality is crucial in both psychology and human-computer interaction (HCI) fields, especially in user-centered HCI designs aimed at developing personalized technologies.


However, personality is inherently multi-dimensional and context-dependent. Mischel demonstrates that human behavior varies significantly across situations \cite{mischel2013personality}, while symbolic interactionism explains how individuals adjust their behavior based on social interactions \cite{blumer1986symbolic}. This adaptability shows that people naturally show different aspects of themselves when interacting with people of different personality traits (see Fig. 1). Consequently, comprehensive personality assessment requires observing individuals across diverse interactive contexts. Based on these theoretical foundations, we propose two key conditions for achieving adequate personality representation: multiplicity, which captures behavioral variations across different situations, and interactivity, which facilitates natural expression of authentic behavioral patterns.


Traditional personality assessment methods such as standardized questionnaires (e.g., the Big Five Personality Test \cite{john1999big}) can evaluate multiple dimensions but are often influenced by social desirability bias \cite{kreitchmann2019controlling}. Other methods, such as physiological measurements (e.g., EEG, fMRI), attempt to offer objectivity, but the interpretation of multi-dimensional data remains a challenge \cite{li2020quantitative, cai2020robust}. Projective techniques and situational tests also face limitations due to their reliance on subjective interpretation and the difficulty of creating diverse and reliable scenarios \cite{weiner2001advancing, schultheiss2001assessment, mussel2016situational}. The common problem with the above methods is the lack of sufficient elicitation and analysis of multi-faceted personality representation, as well as the inability to complete the assessment process naturally in an imperceptible way. In contrast, gamified methods showed great potential to solve the above challenges, because they provide opportunities to assess personality in diverse and naturally interactive scenarios, achieving a valid assessment \cite{harman2024advances, weidner2019playing, wu2022individual}. However, previous studies still lack powerful tools to effectively analyze complex data related to personality.



In recent years, the development of large language models (LLMs) such as GPT-4 \cite{achiam2023gpt}, QWen 2 \cite{yang2024qwen2}, and Llama 3.1 \cite{dubey2024llama} has provided possibilities for fully considering multiplicity and interactivity factors in personality assessment. On the one hand, LLMs can be used to create interactive agents that can simulate various human personalities \cite{xie2024can,xu2024character} through prompts and fine-tuning methods \cite{huang2023revisiting,liu2024dynamic}. This capability, combined with the Media Equation theory \cite{reeves1996media} which suggests people naturally apply social interaction rules to computers, enables LLM agents to simulate situational diversity and guide users to show multiple facets of their personality in natural interactions. On the other hand, recent studies have explored the use of LLM as analytical tools for personality assessment \cite{lee2024chatfive,rao2023can,yang-etal-2024-psychogat}, with promising initial results in providing advanced intelligence to analyze personality-related data \cite{li2024eerpd,peters2024large,zhang2024can}. However, these studies often overlook the role of situational diversity, which may limit the comprehensiveness and accuracy of personality assessments.


Therefore, this study aims to focus on the multiplicity of personality representation and develop a gamified interactive method to achieve effective personality assessment in a natural and imperceptible way. The contributions of this study are as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item We propose a novel personality theory inspired framework, Gamified Personality Assessment through Multi-Personality-Representations (Multi-PR GPA), which considers multiplicity of personality in assessment for the first time, and achieves a good validity.

    \item We use LLMs to create agents with distinct personalities and incorporate gamified natural interaction processes. This approach can lead to high immersion and authentic multi-type personality data, enabling effective and imperceptible personality assessment.


    \item We proposed two personality assessment methods based on the Multi-PR GPA framework: Direct Assessment and Que-based Assessment. We found that these two methods have their own advantages in assessing different dimensions of personality.


\end{itemize}


\section{Related Works}


\subsection{Personality and Its Multiplicity}


Personality refers to the integrated structure of psychological and physiological systems within an individual, which shape and influence their patterns of behavior, thinking, and emotional responses \cite{allport1961pattern,carver2010personality}. Psychologists have developed various theories to understand personality, such as the Big Five \cite{Briggs_1992,goldberg2013alternative,Raad_2000}, the Sixteen Personality Factor (16PF) \cite{2012,Sells_Cattell}, and the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI) \cite{myers1962myers}, which are widely applied in practice \cite{lounsbury2005investigation}.



As one of the most widely accepted personality theories \cite{john2008paradigm}, the Big Five encompasses five dimensions: Extraversion, Agreeability, Conscientiousness, Neuroticism, and Openness \cite{costa1988personality, goldberg2013alternative}. Each dimension is strongly associated with distinct behavioral tendencies, as demonstrated by the Big Five Inventory (BFI) questionnaire \cite{john1999big}. Besides behaviors, studies show a strong correlation between personality traits and language use in communication \cite{Hirsh_Peterson_2009,Lee_Kim_Seo_Chung_2007,pennebaker2001patterns,pennebaker1999linguistic}. Furthermore, traits such as extraversion and neuroticism are closely related to emotional levels and responses to environmental stimuli \cite{revelle2009personality}. These connections between personality traits and individual differences in behavior, language, and emotion provide a foundation for our approach. Therefore, analyzing multi-type interactive data may help achieve a comprehensive personality assessment.


Moreover, as aforementioned in the introduction, personality is multi-dimensional and context-dependent, an individual's performance in social contexts is determined by both personal characteristics (i.e., dispositional determinants of social behavior) and situational factors (i.e., situational determinants of social behavior) \cite{snyder1985personality}. As a result, an individual's personality traits may manifest in different cognitive, behavioral, and emotional patterns across various contexts, reflecting their complexity and multiplicity. Therefore, it is difficult to fully assess an individual's personality solely through their performance in a single situation. Considering the multifaceted nature of personality expression in different situations is essential for an accurate personality assessment. Furthermore, situations and interactions are correlated \cite{diener1984person}; sufficient interaction is also essential to fully demonstrate the multifaceted representation of personality within situations.




\subsection{Game-Based Assessment}

Game-Based Assessment (GBA) introduces interactive game elements into the assessment process to evaluate individuals' competencies, skills, or knowledge \cite{gomez2022systematic}, and is also known as Gamified Assessment. GBA makes the assessment process more natural \cite{landers2022game, mccord2019game, ramos2024serious} and implicit \cite{denden2018implicit}, while offering advantages by creating diverse interactive situations \cite{elyasi2023mbti, gomez2024developing}. Moreover, its importance in modern personality measurement is gradually being recognized, as it can reduce the resistance typically encountered when individuals engage in traditional personality assessments. Specifically, Ramos-Villagrasa et al. argued that serious games offer validity and reliability in measuring personality traits similar to traditional questionnaires, such as BFI-2-S \cite{ramos2024serious, soto2017next}. Weidner et al. demonstrated that GBA can assess personality traits, as behavior in the game is highly correlated with personality dimensions, such as Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Emotional Stability, and Honesty-Humility \cite{weidner2019playing}. Further research by Wu et al. \cite{wu2022individual} validated the feasibility and effectiveness of GBA in measuring the Big Five personality traits.


However, these studies also highlight the challenges of using traditional GBA to measure the Big Five personality traits, primarily due to the limitations of traditional machine learning methods in handling the large and complex behavioral data generated in games. For example, these methods may struggle to capture the subtleties of traits like Conscientiousness, which involves multiple components such as achievement striving, self-discipline, and caution—each of which may manifest differently depending on the game environment \cite{wu2022individual}. Therefore, it is necessary to adopt more advanced AI techniques to effectively analyze the large volume of data generated during interactions.




\subsection{AI-assisted Personality Assessment}

AI-assisted personality assessment has recently gained widespread attention due to its potential to enhance personalized interactions \cite{Qian_Huang_Zhao_Xu_Zhu_2018,Zhang_Dinan_Urbanek_Szlam_Kiela_Weston_2018}. Researchers have explored various approaches to predict personality traits using different sources of data and AI techniques. Specifically, these approaches include mainly text-based automated personality prediction \cite{bruno2022personality,feizi2021state,mairesse2007using}, and digital footprint-based personality prediction \cite{Farnadi_Zoghbi_Moens_De,Oberlander_Nowson_2006,Skowron_Tkalčič_Ferwerda_Schedl_2016,Tadesse_Lin_Xu_Yang_2018}.

Traditional machine learning models such as SVM and HMM have been widely used for personality prediction based on multimodal data. For example, Gilpin et al. \cite{gilpin2018perception} used voice signals to predict the Big Five personality traits, laying the foundation for personalized and emotionally intelligent conversational agents. Kim et al. \cite{kim2018detecting} combined online data (e.g., Slack chat interactions) and offline data (e.g., office movement patterns) to predict traits such as Extroversion. Berkovsky et al. \cite{berkovsky2019detecting} proposed a framework that integrates eye-tracking data with emotional stimuli to detect multiple personality traits. Although the HCI community has made progress in exploring personality prediction methods through multimodal data integration with classic machine learning models, these approaches still face validity challenges due to the inherent limitations of classic machine learning algorithms in processing complex data. These challenges could be addressed by more powerful models like LLMs.



Recently, some initial studies have leveraged LLMs to decode personality traits from various forms of user-generated text \cite{peters2024large,peters2024large2,zhang2024can}. However, these studies face two main issues: (1) insufficiently structured prompts, leading to underutilization of LLMs' reasoning capabilities; (2) lack of integration of relevant personality assessment knowledge, which often focuses too much on the technical aspects and neglects the theoretical foundations. Other studies have attempted to extract useful knowledge from LLMs to improve the personality assessment capabilities of smaller models \cite{hu2024llm}. Yang et al. \cite{yang2023psycot} combined Chain of Thought (CoT) with traditional personality questionnaire and surpassed fine-tuning approaches to predict personality traits. However, relying on explicit questions from psychological questionnaires to infer personality may overlook many implicit features. For the first time, Li et al. \cite{li2024eerpd} proposed a method based on the Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) framework to incorporate psychological knowledge of emotion regulation into LLM-based personality assessment, which improved prediction accuracy. Rao et al. \cite{rao2023can} developed a LLM-based personality assessment method by constructing unbiased prompts to mitigate bias issues. However, their study primarily focused on effectively assessing the personality of specific group entities (e.g., doctors, teachers) rather than general individuals. Lee et al. \cite{lee2024chatfive} developed the ChatFive system by using LLM-supported personalized dialogue to assess the Big Five personality traits, significantly enhancing user engagement and experience, b but demonstrated insufficient predictive capability for the Neuroticism trait. PsychoGAT \cite{yang-etal-2024-psychogat} is an approach that transforms traditional questionnaire-based assessments into an interactive, game-based assessment framework, addressing participant resistance in psychological tests and achieving high reliability and validity. These studies are still limited by the lack of sufficient interactivity and real-time feedback.






\subsection{LLMs' Personality}
\label{section:llm_personality}
LLM agents are increasingly applied in games, education, and physiological assessment \cite{park2023generative,huang2024far,xu2023exploring,wang2024nemobot,yang-etal-2024-psychogat}. Designing different personality features to them, such as a personalized AI assistant \cite{huang2023humanity}, helps enhance human-likeness and user experience \cite{dillion2023can}.

