LLMs are trained on vast and diverse datasets (e.g., Common Crawl \cite{raffel2020exploring}) from the Internet, which contain a large amount of language expressions from individuals with different personalities. Therefore, it is possible to explore the method of inducing specific personality traits of LLM agents. Recently, LLMs have shown the ability to show a specific average personality that aligns with the statistical distribution observed in human populations \cite{jiang2024evaluating}. The primary methods for inducing personality traits of LLM agents are prompt engineering and fine-tuning. Prompt engineering involves designing specific prompts to guide the model in exhibiting certain personality traits. For example, Jiang et al. \cite{jiang2024evaluating} used prompt engineering to induce the Big Five personality traits in ChatGPT. %and developed the Machine Personality Inventory (MPI) to evaluate the behavior of LLMs using standardized multiple-choice questions. 
Huang et al. \cite{huang2023revisiting} demonstrated that \texttt{gpt-3.5-turbo} can exhibit a variety of personality traits under specific prompt adjustments, showing potential to simulate the multiplicity of human personalities. Serapio-Garcia et al. \cite{serapio2023personality} used language qualifiers commonly found in the Likert scale \cite{Likert} to provide more precise control over personality levels. As to the category of fine-tuning methods, Liu et al. \cite{liu2024dynamic} proposed the Dynamic Personality Generation (DPG) method. This method combines Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models (LoRA) technology with Hypernetworks to generate adapter weights, enabling more flexible fine-tuning of LLMs to induce specific personality traits. In summary, prompt engineering has been widely used to guide models to exhibit personality features, while fine-tuning methods provide more precise and personalized control. This study will utilize these established methods to achieve effective multi-personality simulation.




In general, significant progress has been made in AI-assisted personality assessment, and existing methods demonstrate the potential to accurately predict personality traits. However, these methods also face challenges, such as insufficient interactivity within assessment frameworks and the lack of consideration for contextual/situational diversity. Most assessments are based only on static text (e.g., blogs), lacking the multi-type textual data generated from real-time interactions. Given the advantages of LLMs in natural language processing, multi-modal data integration, and complex data handling, this study considers them as potential tools to address these challenges.



\section{Framework Design}
\label{sec:framework}


Based on previous theory and empirical results on personality, \textbf{multiplicity} and \textbf{interactivity} are two key factors for inducing adequate personality representations. This guides us to propose a novel framework of Gamified Personality Assessment through Multi-Personality-Representations (Multi-PR GPA). The main components and workflow of this framework are illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:fig2}. It contains four components: Gamified Interaction, LLM Agents with Controlled Personality, Multi-type Game Data Perception, and Personality Assessment. By integrating these components based on a personality model, the framework is able to comprehensively assess multiple dimensions of users' personalities in natural interactive scenarios. In this paper, we take the widely accepted Big Five personality theory as a use case to explain this framework. The Big Five model was chosen due to its classic status, solid empirical foundation, reliability, and extensive validation across different cultural contexts \cite{gurven2013universal, benet1998cinco}. 






\subsection{Gamified Interaction}
\label{GI}

As aforementioned in section \ref{Intro}, interactivity is one of the key conditions for achieving adequate personality representation. Fluent and natural interaction can better approach personality representation. Therefore, we adopted a gamified approach (as shown in the orange block of Fig. \ref{fig:fig2}). In our design, through interacting with the agent in the game scenario, the user will express their real personality traits without being aware of being evaluated. In this way, the user's behaviors, language, and decision-making information can be fully induced and collected. 




\subsection{LLM Agents with Controlled Personality}
\label{LAMP}
When individuals face different scenarios/contexts, their personality presentations may also be different \cite{kuper2022individual, piechurska2020personality}. To understand human personality, it is important to collect relevant information across various contexts, such as interacting with people with different personalities. As aforementioned in section \ref{section:llm_personality}, research on LLMs has made significant progress in simulating specific personalities. Therefore, we first use personality prompts to make a specific personality dimension dominant in the LLM Agent (as shown in the pink blocks of Fig. \ref{fig:fig2}). Furthermore, according to the media equation theory \cite{reeves1996media} and the Computers as Social Actors (CASA) paradigm \cite{nass1994computers}, to fully trigger human social responses, interactive agents should be more human-like. To achieve this, we approach the design from a human cognition perspective \cite{sporns2016modular} and have developed four sub-modules that particularly enhance the agent's ability to maintain and express its assigned personality in interactions:

\begin{itemize}
    \item The \textbf{Memory} submodule records not only game outcomes but also personality-relevant interaction patterns, enabling the agent to maintain personality consistency across multiple rounds.
    \item The \textbf{Reflection} submodule evaluates past interactions based on assigned personality traits. This allows an agreeable agent to reflect differently on a user's defection (emphasizing relationship repair) compared to a neurotic agent (focusing on anticipated negative outcomes).
    \item The \textbf{Reasoning} submodule applies personality-specific decision processes. For example, openness-dominant agents consider more creative possibilities, but conscientious agents methodically weigh options against rules and patterns.
    \item The \textbf{Planning} submodule generates strategies that consistently reflect the agent's personality profile. For example, extraversion-dominant agents prioritize engaging interaction over purely strategic outcomes, while neurotic agents might develop more cautious, risk-averse approaches.
\end{itemize}

Through this design, each agent maintains a coherent personality throughout the interaction, creating distinct situational contexts that effectively elicit the user's varied personality presentations.






\subsection{Multi-Type Game Perception}
\label{MTTP}

To comprehensively assess personality, it is necessary to fully utilize the information generated during the game's interaction. Therefore, in the Multi-Type Game Perception module (as shown in the blue block of Fig. \ref{fig:fig2}), we proposed four types of textual data related to personality representation:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Text:} The natural dialogue between the user and agent, which captures direct expressions of thoughts and feelings.
\item \textbf{Behavior:} The actions taken by the user, revealing strategic thinking patterns and behavioral tendencies.
\item \textbf{Emotion:} The emotion label extracted from the dialogue.
\item \textbf{Fine-grained personality traits:} Fine-grained personality characteristics extracted from fragmented interactions.
\end{itemize}

The text and behavior data provide the foundational information from which the agent engages in the game and makes decisions. The emotion and fine-grained personality traits represent higher-level information extracted from these basic data. Although current LLMs can handle longer contexts, they struggle to pay attention to detailed information in extended interactions. These nuanced details reflect subtle personality characteristics that might be overlooked. Therefore, by integrating all four types of data, we can create a comprehensive representation of the user's personality.




\subsection{Personality Assessment}
\label{PA}

The Multi-Type Game Perception module provides a rich data source related to personality representation. To integrate these multi-type textual data into LLMs for personality assessment, we designed the workflow of the Personality Assessment (shown in the green block of Fig. \ref{fig:fig2}).

In the personality assessment module, we incorporated expert psychological knowledge and Chain of Thought (CoT) into LLMs to enhance the effectiveness of assessment. This integration is grounded in cognitive psychology principles showing that explicit reasoning paths significantly improve complex judgment tasks involving multi-dimensional data \cite{bialek2016can}. Drawing from personality assessment methodologies in psychology, we proposed designing structured prompts to guide the LLM to evaluate personality traits along standardized dimensions while considering contextual diversity across different agent interactions.

With theoretically-informed structured prompts, CoT allows the LLMs to conduct deeper analysis by first identifying personality-relevant behaviors in each interaction context, then systematically comparing patterns across multiple agent interactions. This method facilitates more precise personality assessments by mitigating the influence of situational biases that typically affect single-context evaluations. Finally, the LLM provides detailed and interpretable analyzes that not only clarify the scores and underlying rationale, but also improve the transparency of the assessment results.




