\subsection{Implementation of Personality Assessment}
% 
Our assessment method is based on interaction data from agents with different personalities (shown on the left side of Fig. \ref{fig:fig8}). Based on this data, we designed two main approaches for personality assessment: Direct Assessment (DA) and Que-based Assessment (QA) (shown in the green block of Fig. \ref{fig:fig5} and Fig. \ref{fig:fig8}). We proposed these two assessment methods to balance the trade-offs between free-form contextual analysis and structured questionnaire-based evaluation, as different personality dimensions may be better captured through different assessment approaches. The prompts used for both types of assessment are detailed in Appendices \ref{DA} and \ref{QA}. Both DA and QA integrate expert psychological knowledge, CoT, and structured prompts into the LLMs to enhance the accuracy of the assessment. Expert psychological knowledge enables the LLM to understand the characteristics that influence the scoring of each personality dimension, while CoT and structured prompts improve the LLM's performance in personality assessment tasks. These two assessment methods provide detailed and interpretable analyses through Fixed Response Templates and Transformed Questions. They not only provide assessment scores and rationale, but also increase the transparency of the results.

\subsubsection{Direct Assessment}

DA directly evaluates the collected multi-type textual data by inputting it into the LLMs. We used a neutral LLM as the evaluator to minimize any bias that might arise from personality prompts. DA generates assessment results by constraining responses with a fixed template.





\subsubsection{Que-based Assessment}
QA converts traditional questionnaires into peer evaluation forms (purple block in Fig. \ref{fig:fig8}), expecting to enhance the objectivity of the assessment. The LLM evaluates each question based on interaction history, providing reasons for its answers. The scores for each dimension of the Big Five Personality are then calculated according to the scoring rules of the BFI-44.





\subsection{Implementation Under Responsible AI and FATE Guidelines}
\label{sec:fate}
When designing AI systems, it is essential not only to focus on technological innovation, but also to ensure that AI systems are responsible. This requires us to carefully consider \textbf{FATE} (\textit{Fairness}, \textit{Accountability}, \textit{Transparency}, and \textit{Ethics}) issues throughout the research and development process. We fully recognize the importance of the FATE principles and have implemented a series of measures to ensure that our system adheres to these principles. 

In terms of \textbf{Fairness}, the OCEAN model and the BFI-44 questionnaire have been localized and validated in multiple countries and regions \cite{gurven2013universal, benet1998cinco}. We use these validated tools to ensure that personality assessments are fair across different cultural contexts. Furthermore, we have designed prompts to ensure that LLMs evaluate based on facts, thereby minimizing potential biases and improving the fairness of the assessments. To ensure the \textbf{Accountability} in AI system, the LLM's assessment results are evidence-based and traceable. For example, as shown in Fig. \ref{fig:4b}, the personality assessment results are linked to the interaction records. In terms of \textbf{Transparency}, we have demonstrated the effectiveness of each type of data through ablation studies and provided clear explanations of the results of personality assessment. To ensure \textbf{Ethics}, we conducted a strict ethical review process, ensuring that all participants were informed of potential risks before the experiment and voluntarily consented to participate. In addition, we strictly adhere to privacy protection and data confidentiality principles to ensure the safeguarding of all personal data.

\section{User study}

To validate the effectiveness of the proposed Multi-PR GPA framework, we conducted a user study. Specifically, we examine the important role of multiplicity and interactivity of personality representation in enhancing the performance of personality assessment.

