\subsection{Study Design}


In our experimental process, users interacted with five agents. Based on these interaction data, we designed two experimental analysis conditions:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Interacting with a single agent (O/C/E/A/N)}: Users interacted with one agent, where the agent exhibited only one distinct personality trait from the Big Five model. The assessment was based solely on the interactions with this single agent.
\item \textbf{Interacting with multiple agents (ALL)}: Users interacted with five different agents, each representing a different personality trait from the Big Five model. The assessment was conducted by aggregating the interactions across all five agents.
\end{itemize}
We employed two evaluation methods using the LLM agent: Direct Assessment and Que-based Assessment. Furthermore, we compared the performance of two models (\texttt{gpt-4o} and \texttt{gpt-4o-mini}) in the personality assessment tasks. This comparison helped us determine if the larger model offered any significant benefits for personality assessment. Additionally, we conducted an ablation study to examine the importance of different types of textual data. By removing certain data types, we analyzed their impact on the personality assessment accuracy of model.




\subsection{Participants}

Forty-two participants (21 males, 21 females; M = 22.07, SD = 2.32 years) were recruited from a local university. Individuals who had consumed alcohol, experienced severe fatigue, taken medication, or been ill immediately before the experiment were excluded. The study, which posed no harm to participants, adhered to the Declaration of Helsinki and was approved by the Human Research Ethics Committee. All participants gave written informed consent after being told about the general procedures and minimal risks. To avoid bias, the specific purpose (assessing personality traits) was disclosed after the experiment. During debriefing, participants were fully informed, compensated \$10, and given the option to confirm or withdraw data usage consent. All ultimately agreed to the use of their data for research.


\subsection{Measures}

\textbf{The Big Five personality traits} of the participants were measured with the Chinese version of the BFI-44 \cite{john1999bigfive}. This inventory is based on the Big Five Factor Model, covering five core traits: Extraversion, Agreeableness, Conscientiousness, Neuroticism, and Openness. Each item is rated on a five-point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree, 5 = strongly agree), with good reliability and validity. The inventory has been widely used in personality research and has been validated in Chinese samples \cite{carciofo2016psychometric}.


\textbf{The flow experience} is a highly enjoyable mental state in
which the individual is fully immersed and engaged in the
activities \cite{sweetser2005gameflow,czikszentmihalyi1990flow}. It was assessed with the Flow Short-Scale \cite{peifer2021advances}, which consists of 10 items (e.g., "My thoughts/activities run fluidly and smoothly"). Participants rated these items on a 7-point Likert scale (1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree). This scale has been widely adopted in previous studies \cite{bian2020role,bian2018exploring,zhang2024we,shin2013exploring} and proven to be a reliable tool for evaluating flow in virtual environments. In the current study, the scale showed good reliability ($\alpha = 0.792$).

\textbf{Personal Involvement} is operationalized as importance, indicating the perception of situational and/or intrinsic self-relevance to somebody or something \cite{novak2000measuring}. It was measured with a five-item scale (e.g., "important/unimportant") by scoring on a seven-point Likert scale. The scale assesses the importance and relevance of the activity for the participants
. It has been widely used in previous studies \cite{liu2012measuring} and showed good reliability in this study ($\alpha = 0.705$).

\textbf{Social presence}, or the feeling of being there with a "real" person, is a crucial user experience when interacting with a virtual person \cite{oh2018systematic}. It was assessed with a five-item scale (e.g., "I perceive that I am in the presence of another person") by scoring on a 7-point Likert scale. This scale has proven to be a reliable tool for evaluating the social presence \cite{felnhofer2014physical} and demonstrated strong reliability in this study ($\alpha = 0.829$).





\subsection{Task and Procedure}

As shown in Fig. \ref{fig:fig4}, the experimental procedure included three main phases: \textbf{Before Game}, \textbf{During Game}, and \textbf{After Game}. Each phase includes specific tasks such as filling out questionnaires, reading instructions, interacting with various personality agents during the game, and participating in post-test evaluations and interviews. The colors represent different activities and agents, with each color corresponding to a specific task or agent as detailed in the legend. In the actual game, the sequence of agent personalities is randomized. The duration of each game round is determined by the number of dialogue exchanges, which is under the user's control. The user can decide how many times to converse per round and can terminate the dialogue at any time. Each interaction with an agent consists of six rounds, and each round includes both a dialogue phase and a decision phase. We chose six rounds based on small-scale user testing in the development phase, where interactions lasted around ten minutes, keeping users engaged without boredom. Our user experience results supported this (Sec \ref{RUE}).



