\subsubsection{Results of Comparison Experiment}

The experimental results are shown in Table \ref{tab:compare}, which presents the personality assessment performance of the LLM agent under different experimental conditions: Interacting with a single Agent (O/C/E/A/N) and Interacting with multiple Agents (All). We used \texttt{gpt-4o} and \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} to implement two assessment methods (Direct Assessment and Que-based Assessment), and their performance under these conditions is also presented in Table \ref{tab:compare}. The main findings are summarized as follows:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Evaluations under the Interacting with multiple Agents (All) condition performed better in terms of RMSE and MAE.} This suggests that personality assessments conducted across multiple contexts can more comprehensively capture users' external personality traits, further validating the effectiveness of a multi-dimensional, multi-context approach for AI-assisted personality assessment. 
    \item \textbf{Direct Assessment and Que-based Assessment each have their strengths across different personality dimensions.} For instance, Direct Assessment performs better in the Extraversion dimension, while Que-based Assessment achieves lower errors in the Openness, Conscientiousness, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism dimensions. This indicates that different assessment methods have varying applicability in capturing specific personality traits, and the most appropriate assessment strategy should be chosen based on the specific task. We have discussed this further in the discussion Section \ref{section:ques}.
    \item \textbf{The performance of \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} is generally better than \texttt{gpt-4o}.} This is an unexpected result given that \texttt{gpt-4o} has stronger reasoning capabilities. We believe this may be due to over-reasoning by \texttt{gpt-4o} in certain contexts. We discussed this in detail in the discussion Section \ref{section:llm4o}.

\end{itemize}

In addition to comparing different LLMs and assessment methods, we also compared our method with existing personality assessment methods and examined gender differences. The relevant experimental results are presented in Section \ref{comp} and \ref{gender}.

\subsubsection{Results of Ablation Study}

To further investigate the importance and effectiveness of different types of information in personality assessment, we conducted an ablation study using \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} for Direct Assessment, with the results shown in Table \ref{tab:ablation}. Our main observations are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Under Interacting with multiple Agents (All) condition, removing nearly any type of text led to an increase in the error of personality assessment}, indicating that the Emotion and fine-grained Personality traits information has a significant positive impact on overall personality assessment.
    \item \textbf{In certain cases, adding certain types of textual data actually led to an increase in error.} For example, in the Neuroticism dimension, the addition of the Emotion data resulted in an increase in personality assessment error when interacting with a single-personality agent. This could be due to the current LLM's limitations in accurately classifying the emotional states of certain sentences, leading to some mislabeling of emotional tags. Nevertheless, the emotional tags assigned by the LLM generally correctly reflect the expressed emotions, as supported by the performance in the other four dimensions. Another possible reason is that the fine-grained Personality traits data already includes some emotional features (this is discussed in detail in the discussion section, see Section \ref{section:error}).
    \item \textbf{After removing the Personality traits data (P), errors increased in most cases, indicating that the fine-grained Personality traits data can indeed enhance the accuracy of assessing certain personality traits.} However, in the Agreeableness dimension, the error actually decreased, suggesting that in specific contexts, the Personality traits data may introduce additional information or complexity that is not fully relevant to the Agreeableness dimension, leading to higher assessment errors. Notably, under the Interacting with multiple Agents (All), the removal of the Personality traits data led to an increase in error, indicating that when considering all personality traits comprehensively, the Personality traits data remains crucial for providing consistent and holistic assessments. 
\end{itemize}

Overall, the Text (dialogue) and Behavior data provide foundational information, while the addition of fine-grained Personality traits and Emotion data enhances assessment accuracy. The combination of all types of textual data (T+B+P+E) yields the best performance across most personality dimensions, demonstrating that integrating multiple types of textual information can offer the most comprehensive and accurate personality trait assessments. This further underscores the necessity and effectiveness of integrating multiple information sources in personality assessment tasks.


\section{Discussion}
In this section, we summarize the most important findings of our method and highlight some potential future directions for personality assessment.

\subsection{Framework Feasibility}

Personality is inherently multi-faceted and context-dependent, making comprehensive personality representation difficult to capture through single-context interactions. We identified two essential conditions for adequate personality representation: multi-dimensionality and interactivity. Guided by Media Equation Theory \cite{reeves1996media} and the CASA paradigm \cite{nass1994computers}, agents with human-like features can help achieve these conditions. Based on these conditions, we propose an imperceptible personality assessment method utilizing gamified interactions between users and multi-personality agents.

In Section \ref{sec:framework}, we present the Multi-PR GPA framework. To evaluate this framework's feasibility, we implemented a prototype system based on the Big Five personality model and our designed trust game. Our assessment focused on two key aspects: multiplicity and interactivity. For multiplicity, comparison and ablation experiments demonstrated that our multi-situation approach achieves significantly better assessment performance compared to single-situation approaches, confirming the importance of considering multiple personality facets. For interactivity, we measured user experience in the interaction process, which validated the significance of interactive assessment. Through these experiments, we successfully validated the feasibility of our framework.

\subsection{Experimental Analysis of Multi-PR GPA}
\subsubsection{Interaction fluency}

The Media Equation Theory suggests that people respond naturally and socially to media. This theory aligns with the conceptual design of our research. To verify this, we measured three dimensions during the post-test phase: flow experience, personal involvement, and social presence during the interaction process. The results showed that the participants reported high levels of flow experience, demonstrating the fluidity of the interaction process. Furthermore, personal involvement and social presence measurements not only verified the smoothness of the interaction but also indicated that the personality traits and cognitive processes that we designed for the agent made users perceive authentic social interaction. According to Media Equation Theory, even the most basic computer media can trigger human social responses. Therefore, we believe that the moderate to high levels exhibited by users in 'perceiving the presence of real people and emotional investment' during the experiment further ensured that users generated the expected social responses to the agent, thus supporting our design of using agent personalities to simulate different interactive scenarios.
\subsubsection{The Choice between Direct Assessment and Que-based Assessment.}
\label{section:ques}
In Interacting with multiple Agents condition, Que-based Assessment outperforms Direct Assessment in evaluating Conscientiousness, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism, whereas Direct Assessment shows better results for Openness and Extraversion. This may be because Openness and Extraversion are more easily expressed through overt behaviors, such as active participation, social interaction, and innovative thinking. In contrast, Que-based Assessment offers a more systematic approach that allows the LLM to more comprehensively reflect on the internal traits expressed by users, thus mitigating potential biases introduced by pre-trained data and addressing limitations that may arise in Direct Assessment of these traits.

These findings suggest that combining different assessment methods in multi-dimensional personality evaluation may be more effective. This hybrid approach can help build a more comprehensive and balanced personality assessment system, thereby improving the accuracy and effectiveness of the evaluations. Future research could explore how to further optimize the integration of these assessment methods to maximize their complementary strengths.

\subsubsection{LLM Model Performance in GPA Task.}
\label{section:llm4o}
We conducted a comparative analysis between \texttt{gpt-4o} and \texttt{gpt-4o-mini}, and to our surprise, \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} generally outperformed \texttt{gpt-4o} in most scenarios. We hypothesize that this may be due to "over-reasoning" by \texttt{gpt-4o}, where the %model's
model attempts to overcomplicate unfamiliar tasks result in suboptimal performance \cite{wu2024easily}. This phenomenon is particularly evident when LLMs engage in excessive complexity in their reasoning processes, leading to poorer outcomes \cite{wu2024reasoning,austin2021program}. In contrast, \texttt{gpt-4o-mini} may have avoided this pitfall by not overcomplicating the reasoning process, thus achieving better performance. 

\subsubsection{Slight Error Increase with Added Types of Textual Data in Some Cases}
\label{section:error}
In our ablation study, we observed that the addition of the emotion data slightly increased the error rate in a few cases. We believe this could be attributed to two main factors: First, although we assigned emotion labels to sentences by considering game rules and context, current LLMs have certain limitations in accurately assigning these labels. For example, the model may struggle to correctly identify complex or mixed emotions. Second, the personality traits data might already include some emotional information, leading to redundancy or even conflict when the LLM incorrectly predicts the emotional state. To address this, we aim to integrate additional types of data, such as voice features during speech \cite{wu2024beyond}, in future work to assist LLMs in more accurately assessing the emotional content of sentences, thereby enhancing the effectiveness of personality assessment.

\subsubsection{Gender Difference}
In our experiment on gender differences, we compared the evaluation results between male and female participants. We found that multi-personality representation achieved excellent results for both male and female participants. However, in certain dimensions, such as for the Neuroticism dimension in males, interacting with an agent exhibiting Extraversion may prove more effective. This suggests the potential impact of gender on personality assessment. In future work, we will further explore these differences by incorporating gender differences knowledge to better achieve accurate gamified personality assessment.



\subsection{Gamified Personality Assessment Application}
Personality assessment is important in predicting human behavior, understanding individual cognition, and facilitating social interactions. It provides a more accurate understanding of an individual's motivations and emotional responses in different contexts, which is highly valuable in areas such as education, career development, mental health, and intelligent system design. However, traditional personality assessments are often explicit (participants aware that they are being evaluated), which may lead to social desirability bias. Additionally, expert interviews or situational tests are resource-intensive, limiting their scalability.

To address these issues, we propose an implicit gamified personality assessment method. This approach not only captures an individual’s true personality traits in various contexts through more natural interactions but also offers multiple practical applications. For example, in education, it can help schools build psychological profiles of students, which allows for the design of personalized learning plans. In recruitment and talent management, personality assessment can be used to better understand candidates' personality traits, making it easier to find people who fit the company’s culture and teams. In psychological counseling, this method can complement expert interviews by providing a more comprehensive understanding of the individual's true personality, reducing self-defense mechanism. Finally, in interactive intelligent agent systems, implicit personality assessment can help create more human-centered interactions, enhancing systems' understanding of individual preferences and behaviors. This leads to more efficient and personalized services, such as personalized recommendation systems, personal assistants, and game NPC design.

\subsection{Bias and Ethical Risks in Implicit Personality Assessment}

In Section \ref{sec:fate}, we introduced how our system mitigate bias under the guidance of Responsible AI and FATE principles, ensuring fairness, accountability, transparency and ethics. However, we have still observed that ChatGPT exhibits a positive bias \cite{ray2023chatgpt} in personality assessments, which indicates a tendency to assign socially desirable scores to specific personality dimensions. This observation aligns with the findings of Derner et al. \cite{derner2024can}. This bias may be a result of reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) \cite{christiano2017deep, stiennon2020learning}, which guides the model towards more positive or neutral responses \cite{casper2023open, xiao2024algorithmic}. We anticipate that this bias issue will be better mitigated with advancements in the Natural Language Processing field. Beyond bias, the abuse/misuse of implicit assessments may bring some risk, such as privacy issues. To address these issues, strict ethical reviews should be established, with clear guidelines for usage scenarios. For example, this technology must only be enabled during gameplay after obtaining user consent. These measures can mitigate the ethical risks associated with this technology, ensuring its responsible application and preventing potential harm to users.


Our research aims to achieve more personalized interactive experiences while enabling better personality assessment that helps users understand themselves and supports career planning and personal development. By designing a system that respects privacy, obtains proper consent, and follows FATE principles, we can harness the potential of gamified personality assessment to provide genuine value to users while addressing legitimate ethical concerns. To mitigate risks such as privacy issues, we conducted strict ethical reviews, and enabled this technology only after obtaining explicit user consent. This approach allowed us to balance technological innovation with ethical responsibility, ensuring that advances in AI-assisted personality assessment benefit individuals without compromising their rights or wellbeing.



\subsection{Limitations and Future Directions}

Some points remain to be addressed in future studies.

First, continue exploring GPA through multi-personality representation. Currently, by interacting with multiple agents exhibiting different personalities, we can induce the user's personality presentation across different dimensions. In the future, we can design more diverse and targeted game scenarios. For example, puzzle games or exploratory tasks could be used to better measure openness and creativity, thereby enhancing the induction of multi-dimensional personality presentation.

Second, further explore the applicability of our Multi-PR GPA framework in other personality assessment tasks. As an exploratory study, we have focused on evaluating the Big Five personality traits. In the future, this can be expanded to include assessments based on 16PF and MBTI.

Finally, increase the diversity of data types. Our current personality assessments are based on multi-type textual data. While these data provide rich information on personality traits, they are still limited. Recent advancements in LLM technology have enabled the processing of additional modalities, such as images. However, our current framework has not yet incorporated other modalities like speech and video. Future work should consider the fusion of multi-modal data (e.g., facial expressions combined with emotional analysis from speech) to enhance the comprehensiveness and accuracy of personality assessments.



\section{Conclusion}


This study proposes a Multi-PR GPA framework for effectively assessing personality implicitly in a game environment by engaging users in interactions with multi-personality agents. To validate this framework, we implemented a prototype system based on the Big Five personality model within a trust game context. Using LLMs to simulate agents with distinct traits for each Big Five dimension, we conducted a user study with 42 participants. From this, we derived the following key conclusions and insights:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Considering multiplicity is important in personality assessment:} Through comparison experiments, we demonstrate that our multi-situation approach achieves better assessment performance compared to single-situation approaches, validating the importance of multiplicity.
    \item \textbf{Gamification successfully creates natural assessment conditions}: Our user experience measurements demonstrate that the gamified approach created high engagement levels while reducing assessment awareness, potentially mitigating social desirability bias.
    \item \textbf{The full utilization of interaction data will enhance the effectiveness of the assessment:} We extracted four types of textual data from interactions. Through an ablation study, we identify the contributions of different types of textual data to the accuracy of the assessment, finding that the integration of all types of text provides the most comprehensive and accurate personality assessments.
    \item \textbf{Different assessment methods complement each other:} Our experiments show Direct Assessment better evaluates Extraversion traits, while Que-based Assessment better captures Openness, Conscientiousness, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism. This suggests a hybrid approach could yield more comprehensive personality evaluations.
\end{itemize}










