Table \ref{tab:essays} presents the results of Multi-PR-GPA compared to several baselines using LLMs. Multi-PR-GPA achieves superior performance on most personality traits, demonstrating the effectiveness of multi-personality presentation. However, its performance on Conscientiousness is lower than that of Zero-Shot CoT. This may be due to the weaker correlation between conscientiousness and language or behavior in our task. This observation is consistent with Tackman’s finding that conscientiousness has less influence on language use compared to other traits \cite{tackman2020personality}.
% 




\section{Gender Differences}
\label{gender}
Table \ref{tab:gender} presents the results of our experiment on gender differences. The main findings are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Multi-personality representation achieves the best performance under most conditions, particularly in the dimensions of Conscientiousness, Extraversion, and Agreeableness, further demonstrating the effectiveness of our framework.
    \item For the Openness dimension, the evaluation results for male participants show little difference between the DA and QA methods (bolded values). However, QA outperforms DA in assessing female participants. This may be because females tend to express Openness trait through language and emotional communication, whereas males often express it through proposing innovative game strategy. In the game environment, the LLM in DA may misinterpret females’ expressions of Openness as being related to Extraversion or Agreeableness. QA, with the support of questionnaires, captures deeper aspects of females’ Openness, resulting in better performance.
    \item For the Neuroticism dimension in males, interacting with an agent exhibiting Extraversion may prove more effective. This is particularly evident in the DA-male and QA-male columns, where the error for male participants under the Extraversion condition is smaller than that for female participants. 
\end{itemize}


