\begin{abstract}
Here we apply the commonly used Rayleigh--Sommerfeld diffraction integral to the propagation of relativistic fields in spacetime, using the theory to find the propagators for both the Klein--Gordon equation and the Dirac equation. Based on these results, we find an interpretation of the Klein--Gordon equation in terms of the Feynman checkerboard model, as well as highlighting some apparently overlooked aspects of the Feynman checkerboard model as it is usually applied to the Dirac equation.



\end{abstract}


\maketitle


\vspace{2pc}
\noindent{\it Keywords}: propagator, Klein--Gordon equation, Dirac equation, Rayleigh--Sommerfeld diffraction integral \\


\section{Introduction}
Inspired by Dirac's discovery of a connection between the Lagrangian in classical mechanics and Heisenberg's matrix mechanics~\cite{Dirac1933}, Feynman proposed the path integral formulation~\cite{Feynman1948} to describe the behavior of matter waves in terms of probability amplitude (or phase change), instead of wave equations, providing an intuitive physical picture on how matter waves propagate through various paths. It has proven to be highly useful even to this day~\cite{junker2024path,benzair2024path,laenen2009path,hertz2016path}. The path integral shows that the matter wave changes its phase in accordance with the path it travels, the phase of each path proportional to the Hamiltonian action of the particle.

There is a similar situation in optics, in diffraction theory, where the optical wave also changes its phase depending on the path it travels. Although the diffraction theory is usually applied in 3D space with time neglected, it can, as a consequence of the divergence theorem of arbitrary dimensions, be extended to spacetime. This is simply a mathematical result, and we can see in e.g. Ref.~\cite{brukner1997diffraction}, that Kirchhoff diffraction theory can be extended in this manner to treat propagation in spacetime. Diffraction theory, when extended to the time dimension, has found wide-ranging applications in recent research~\cite{goussev2012huygens,goussev2013diffraction,barbier2022phase,jones2015path,beau2015three}.  Likewise, as we show here, the Rayleigh diffraction theory can also be extended to propagation in spacetime.  Based on this connection between the path integral and diffraction theory, we use Rayleigh diffraction theory to construct the propagators for the Klein--Gordon~\cite{KleinEqn} and Dirac equations~\cite{DiracEqn}. 

In what follows we shall compare the results of our approach to a different method for calculating the propagator, given by Feynman~\cite{FeynmanQM}, for the particular case of the $1+1$D Dirac equation.  This is known as the Feynman checkerboard model and differs from the sum over paths applied to the Schr\"{o}dinger equation~\cite{SchrodingerEqn}.  Note that this checkerboard approach has been extended to the full Dirac equation in $3+1$ dimensions, see e.g. Ref.~\cite{ord2023feynman}.  We shall show that, using our approach, we reclaim the checkerboard propagator but with additional terms that are non--zero only on the light cone, which are essential to reclaim the correct zero mass limit.  Further to this we apply our method to calculate the propagator for the Klein--Gordon equation, finding a new checkerboard interpretation for this relativistic wave equation, containing two time--reversed copies of the particle motion.




\section{The Propagators for the Klein--Gordon and Dirac Equations}



In quantum mechanics we have a wave equation that involves only a first--order time derivative, namely,
\begin{equation}
\rmi\hbar\partial_t\Psi=\hat{H}\Psi.\label{eq:schrodinger}
\end{equation}
The propagator, $K$, is the integral operator that performs the time evolution according to \cref{eq:schrodinger}, taking any state at time $t'$ to its later form at time $t''$.  This is given by the matrix element of the time evolution operator,
\begin{equation}
K(x'',t'';x',t')=\langle x''|\rme^{\frac{t''-t'}{\rmi\hbar}\hat{H}}|x'\rangle.\label{eq:feynman_propagator}
\end{equation}
We note that here ``propagator'' does not mean the Green function of \cref{eq:schrodinger}, but as stated, the integral kernel $K(b,a)$, which is applied to any initial state $\Psi(a)$ as follows: $\Psi(b)=\int_a K(b,a)\Psi(a)dx$, where $a$ and $b$ denote two events in spacetime.  Feynman discovered~\cite{Feynman1948,FeynmanQM} that this propagator can be expressed in terms of the Hamiltonian action  of the particle, $\mathfrak{S}$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Feynman_PathInt}
    K(x_A,x_B)=\underset{\mbox{all paths}}{\int_{x_B}^{x_A}}\exp\left({\frac\rmi\hbar\mathfrak{S}[x(t)]}\right)\mathcal{D}x(t),
\end{equation}
where throughout we use the compressed notation e.g. $x_{A}$ to indicate \emph{all} coordinates specifying the point $A$, and $\mathcal{D}x$ is understood as an infinite product of infinitesimals, each associated with an integral over a position along the path $x(t)$.  This sum over classical paths is known as a path integral. Conversely, the Hamiltonian action can be given, in terms of the propagator, as~\cref{eq:Feynman_PathInt}
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{cl}
    \mathfrak{S}[l]&=-\rmi\hbar\ln\left(\underset{\Delta \bm{x}\to0}{\lim}\underset{\bm{x}\in l}{\prod}K(\bm{x}+\Delta \bm{x},\bm{x})\right)\\
    &=-\rmi\hbar{\int_{\bm{x}\in l} \ln K(\bm{x}+d\bm{x},\bm{x})},
\end{array}\label{eq:action}
\end{equation}
where $l$ is a path in spacetime.

Interestingly, in classical wave optics, there is a similar concept to the path integral: the idea of optical path length. Indeed, the optical path length is treated as the Hamiltonian action of light.  In classical wave optics, the optical wave is governed by the Helmholtz equation 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:HelmholtzEq}
    (\nabla^2+k^2)\Psi=0.
\end{equation}
Diffraction theory then tells us that we can determine the wave at the point $x_A=(x'',y'',z'')$ by knowing the wave distribution on a given boundary surface containing the point $x_B=(x',y',z')$. The relation between $\Psi(x_A)$ and $\Psi(x_B)$ is known as the Rayleigh--Sommerfeld diffraction integral~\cite{born2013principles}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:RS_dif_int}
\Psi(x_A)=\iint_{z'=0}-\hat{\bm z}\cdot\nabla G_R(x_A,x_B)\Psi(x_B){\rm d}x'{\rm d}y',
\end{equation} 
where $G_R=G_{z}-G_{-z}$ is the Rayleigh--Green function, $G$ is the Green function, and where the subscript gives the $z$ coordinate of the observation point.  Here propagation is from the plane $z'=0$, and the subscript `${\parallel}$' indicates the in--plane coordinates. It is obvious in the integral \cref{eq:RS_dif_int} that the kernel $-\hat{\bm z}\cdot\nabla G_R(x_A,x_B)$ acts as a propagator similar to \cref{eq:feynman_propagator}, with the coordinate $z$ interpreted as a dimension analogous to time. Using the Huygens–Fresnel principle, we can also envision a physical picture similar to the Feynman path formulation.  Writing $K(x_A,x_B)=-\hat{\bm z}\cdot\nabla G_R(x_A,x_B)$, and defining an effective action via \cref{eq:action} we can write the diffraction integral \cref{eq:RS_dif_int} in terms of an integration over paths,
\begin{equation}
    -\hat{\bm z}\cdot\nabla G_R(x_A,x_B)=\underset{\mbox{all paths}}{\int_{x_B}^{x_A}}\exp\left({\frac\rmi\hbar\mathfrak{S}[\boldsymbol{x}_{\parallel}(z)]}\right)\mathcal{D}\boldsymbol{x}_{\parallel}(z)\label{eq:rayleigh-path}
\end{equation}
From \cref{eq:RS_dif_int,eq:rayleigh-path}, we can see there is a general connection between the Green function of a wave equation and the path integral representation of the propagator.

Now, let us explore how to find the propagator and path integral with the idea developed above. In the diffraction theory, we manage to construct such a function $F(\Psi,G)$, e.g. $-\hat{\bm z}\cdot\nabla G_R(x_A,x_B)$ in \cref{eq:RS_dif_int}, with $\Psi$ the wave and $G$ the Green's function, that satisfies the following equation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Key_Idea}
\begin{array}{cl}
     \Psi(x_A)&=\int_V \delta(x_A-x_B)\Psi(x_B){\rm d}^nx \\
     &=\int_V \mathfrak{L}[G(x_A-x_B)]\Psi(x_B){\rm d}^nx\\
     &=\int_V {\rm{div}}(F(\Psi,G)){\rm d}^nx\\
     &=\oint_{\partial V}(F(\Psi,G), {\rm d}^{(n-1)}\bm{x})
\end{array}
\end{equation}
where the corresponding wave equation is given abstractly as $\mathfrak{L}[\Psi]=0$ and $G$ is the Green's function of the linear operator $\mathfrak{L}$. We can see from \cref{eq:Key_Idea} that $F(\Psi,G)$ plays a role as a kind of ``current". With this speculation, we can introduce a new parameter $\lambda$ to play a role as ``time'' so that the wave equation becomes 
$$
\rmi\partial_\lambda \Psi=\mathfrak{L}[\Psi]=0.
$$
 But remember that this parameter is not necessary. Similar to the 
Schr\"{o}dinger equation, the``probability" density $\rho$ is set to be $\Psi^\dagger\Psi$ and the unknown function $F$ is then defined by the current $J=-\rmi F(\Psi,\Psi^\dagger)$. We thus obtain a ``continuity" equation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:continuity}
    \frac{\partial \Psi^\dagger\Psi}{\partial\lambda}+{\rm div}(-\rmi F(\Psi,\Psi^\dagger))=0
\end{equation}
which gives us a way to compute the unknown function $F$ via
\begin{equation}\label{eq:divF}
    {\rm div}(-\rmi F(\Psi,\Psi^\dagger))=-\left(\rmi(\mathfrak{L}[\Psi])^\dagger\Psi-\rmi\Psi^\dagger\mathfrak{L}[\Psi]\right).
\end{equation}
As we can see from \cref{eq:Key_Idea}, we shall replace $\Psi^\dagger$ with Green's function $G^\dagger$ in \cref{eq:divF}, giving the explicit form of the divergence of the function $F(\Psi,G)$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:F_Psi_G}
\begin{array}{cl}
     {\rm div}(F(\Psi,G^\dagger))&= (\mathfrak{L}[G])^\dagger\Psi-G^\dagger\mathfrak{L}[\Psi]=(\mathfrak{L}[G])^\dagger\Psi
\end{array}
\end{equation}
which gives $\delta(x_A-x_B)\Psi(x_B)$ that we need in \cref{eq:Key_Idea}. Sometimes, the function $F(\Psi,G)$ does not directly yield the propagator, but it contains the propagator. To better illustrate, let us take the Helmholtz equation $(\nabla^2+k^2)\Psi=0$ as an example. From \cref{eq:divF}, we can obtain directly
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Helmholtz_continuity}
    {\rm div}(-\rmi F(\Psi,\Psi^*))=\rmi\nabla\cdot(\Psi^*\nabla\Psi-\Psi\nabla\Psi^*).
\end{equation}
By replacing the $\Psi^*$ with the conjugate Green's function $G^*$ of the Helmholtz equation in \cref{eq:Helmholtz_continuity} just as what we did in \cref{eq:F_Psi_G}, we obtain the expected function 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Helmholtz_F}
    F(\Psi,G^*)=-(G^*\nabla\Psi-\Psi\nabla  G^*).
\end{equation}
which satisfies $\nabla\cdot F(\Psi,G)=\delta(x_A-x_B)\Psi(x_B)$. Without loss of generality, let $x_A$ locate in the upper hemisphere. Assuming the wave satisfies the Sommerfeld radiation condition, we denote the upper hemisphere as $V$ and construct use the Rayleigh--Green function which vanishes on the screen surface. Although the Rayleigh--Green function is not a Green's function of $\mathfrak{L}$ since $\mathfrak{L}[G_{R}]=\delta_{z_0}-\delta_{-z_0}$, the delta function $\delta_{-z_0}$ lies outside the integral volume $V$ and we still have 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:RS_prototype}
\Psi(x_A)=\int_V \mathfrak{L}[G_R(x_A-x_B)]\Psi(x_B){\rm d}^3 x_B
\end{equation}
which is in accordance with \cref{eq:Key_Idea} and leads to the Rayleigh--Sommerfeld diffraction integral \cref{eq:RS_dif_int}.

\subsection{Propagator and a Feynman checkerboard model for the Klein--Gordon equation}

Similarly, we can extend this diffraction theory to spacetime, applying this method to the Klein--Gordon equation: $(\partial_\mu\partial^\mu+\rmm^2\rmc^2\hbar^{-2})\Psi=0$~\cite{KleinEqn,GordonEqn}. The Klein--Gordon equation is just the Helmholtz equation \cref{eq:HelmholtzEq} but in the spacetime domain. For the Klein--Gordon equation, the $F(\Psi,G)$ function appearing in \cref{eq:Key_Idea} is therefore
\begin{equation}\label{eq:KleinGordon_F}
    F(\Psi,G)=\Psi\partial^\mu G-G\partial^\mu\Psi
\end{equation}
which is similar to \cref{eq:Helmholtz_F} where the gradient is now $\partial^\mu$. As described above, but now applied to four--dimensional space--time, we can apply the same trick to construct the Rayleigh--Green function, $G_R$ which now vanishes on the $t=0$ plane, as illustrated in \cref{fig:RayleighTrick}. 
\begin{figure}[htb!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{Rayleigh_trick.jpg}
    \caption{\textbf{Rayleigh's trick}: a Green function which vanishes on the boundary $t=0$
    can be found through including a mirrored source so that the term $G\partial^\mu\Psi$ disappears in the diffraction integral given in \cref{eq:KleinGordon_F}.}
    \label{fig:RayleighTrick}
\end{figure}
The Rayleigh--Green function for the Klein--Gordon equation then equals
\begin{equation}\label{eq:G_R_KGEq}
    G_{R}=G_{t}-G_{-t},
\end{equation}
where $G$ is the Green function for the Klein--Gordon equation for a source at some fixed point in spacetime, and the subscript `$t$' indicates the time at which it is evaluated, analogous to the $z$ coordinate in Rayleigh's diffraction theory described below \cref{eq:RS_dif_int}.  The Rayleigh--Green function in \cref{eq:G_R_KGEq} vanishes on the $t=0$ plane as expected.  Integrating over the upper spacetime hemisphere as described in the text preceding \cref{eq:RS_prototype}, we have
\begin{equation*}
    \Psi(x_A)=-\frac{1}{\rmc}\iiint_{t_B=0} (\partial_tG_R)\Psi(x_B){\rm d}x{\rm d}y{\rm d}z,
\end{equation*}
which, via the same argument as given before, implies that the propagator for the Klein--Gordon equation is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:KleinGordonEq_K}
    K(x_A-x_B)=-\frac{1}{\rmc}\partial_t G_R(x_A-x_B).
\end{equation}
Here we take the propagator \cref{eq:KleinGordonEq_K} to be causal and thus use a combination of the retarded, $G_+$, and advanced, $G_-$ Green functions in the definition of the Rayleigh--Green function~\cref{eq:G_R_KGEq} as follows,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:KGEq_RGfunc}
    G_R=G_+(t_A-t_B,\bm r)-G_-(-t_A-t_B,\bm r) .
\end{equation}
where ${\bm r}={\bm r}_{A}-{\bm r}_{B}$ is the three dimensional separation vector between the points $A$ and $B$.  The expressions for the retarded and advanced Green functions appearing in \cref{eq:KGEq_RGfunc} are~\cite{scharf2014finite},
\begin{equation}\label{eq:KGEq_G_ret}
  G_+(\tau,\bm r)=\frac{\rmm\rmc^3}{4\pi\hbar s}\theta\left(\tau-\frac r\rmc\right)J_1\left(\frac{\rmm\rmc}\hbar s\right)-\frac{\rmc^2}{2\pi}\delta\left(\tau-\frac r\rmc\right),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:KGEq_G_adv}
    G_-(\tau,\bm r)=\frac{\rmm\rmc^3}{4\pi\hbar s}\theta\left(-\tau-\frac r\rmc\right)J_1\left(\frac{\rmm\rmc}\hbar s\right)-\frac{\rmc^2}{2\pi}\delta\left(\tau+\frac r\rmc\right).
\end{equation}
where  $\theta$ is the Heaviside step function and $J_1$ the first--order Bessel function of the first kind. 
 Substituting the Rayleigh--Green function \cref{eq:KGEq_RGfunc} into \cref{eq:KleinGordonEq_K}, we obtain the $3+1$D propagator for the Klein--Gordon equation. 

In the case of the $1+1$D Klein--Gordon equation, the retarded and the advanced Green's functions will instead be given by (see the appendix),
\begin{equation}\label{eq:KGEq_RAGreen}
    G_\pm(\tau,\Delta x)=\frac14\theta\left(\pm\tau-\frac{|\Delta x|}{\rmc}\right)J_0\left(\frac{\rmm\rmc}\hbar s\right).
\end{equation}
Substituting \cref{eq:KGEq_RAGreen} into \cref{eq:KGEq_RGfunc}, we also find the propagator \cref{eq:KleinGordonEq_K} for the $1+1$D Klein--Gordon equation,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:11KGEq_K}
\begin{array}{cl}
K=&-\frac14\left\{\frac1\rmc\delta\left((t_A-t_B)-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)\right.
\\
&-\theta\left((t_A-t_B)-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)J_1\left(\frac{\rmm\rmc}\hbar s_-\right)\frac{\rmm\rmc(t_A-t_B)}{\hbar s_-}
\\
&+\frac1\rmc\delta\left((-t_A-t_B)-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)
\\
&\left. +\theta\left((-t_A-t_B)-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)J_1\left(\frac{\rmm\rmc}\hbar s_+\right)\frac{\rmm\rmc(t_A+t_B)}{\hbar s_+}\right\}
\end{array}   
\end{equation}
where we have introduced the compressed notation $s_-^2=\rmc^2(t_A-t_B)^2-\Delta x^2$ and $s_+^2=\rmc^2(t_A+t_B)^2-\Delta x^2$. For a massless particle, i.e. $\rmm=0$, the propagator \cref{eq:11KGEq_K} reduces to a sum of delta functions, implying free propagation along the light cone. It should be noted that the Dirac equation can be rewritten in the form of Maxwell's equations \cite{Li_2020} and a set of `electronic fields' $f,g,\bm\varphi,\bm\chi$ as explained in Ref.\cite{Li_2020}.  These fields satisfy the Klein--Gordon equation, which means the above propagator \cref{eq:KleinGordonEq_K} can also be applied to the Dirac equation, providing a different way to construct the path integral for the Dirac wave. 



Additionally, the Klein--Gordon propagator \cref{eq:11KGEq_K} can be interpreted via the Feynman checkerboard model---a commonly used path integral model for the Dirac equation---provided we neglect the $\delta$ function terms localized on the light cone.  We will return to this connection after introducing the Feynman checkerboard model and discussing the propagator for the Dirac equation.





\subsection{The Feynman checkerboard model and the propagator for the Dirac equation}

We shall use the same argument to construct the propagator for the Dirac equation~\cite{DiracEqn} $(\rmi\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu-\rmm\rmc/\hbar)\Psi=0$. But before constructing the propagator, let us first recap Feynman's method for computing the path integral for the Dirac equation.  Feynman used a checkerboard model (illustrated in \cref{fig:FCM}) to construct the propagator, which is very different from the approach applied to the Schr\"{o}dinger equation.
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{FCM.jpg}
    \caption{\textbf{The Feynman checkerboard model}: The red line is the projection of the light cone on the right half $x$--$t$ plane. Here we only plot the first quadrant of the $x$--$t$ plane for convenience.}
    \label{fig:FCM}
\end{figure}
In Feynman's calculation, the particle can only move forward or backward in one dimension at a speed of light, $\rmc$. The particle occasionally reverses its direction at a rate proportional to the rest mass, $\rmm$. This generates a random walk, as sketched in \cref{fig:FCM}. The corresponding wave function of that particle is assumed to change its amplitude by a factor $\rmi M\epsilon$ after each reversal, where $\epsilon$ is the small time step into which the path is discretized.  Thus the propagator $K$ for the Dirac equation is given as~\cite{FeynmanQM}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:FeynmanDef_K}
K=\underset{R}{\sum}N(R)(\rmi \rmM\epsilon)^R,
\end{equation}
where $\rmM=\rmm\rmc/\hbar$, $R$ is the number of reversals or corners on the path illustrated in \cref{fig:FCM}, and $N(R)$ is the total number of paths with $R$ corners. The sum in the propagator \cref{eq:FeynmanDef_K} can---according to Ref.\cite{kull1999path}---be evaluated, giving, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:FeynmanEva_K}
K=\sigma_xJ_0(\rmM s)+\rmi\frac{\rmc \tau}s J_1(\rmM s)+\rmi\frac{\Delta x}s\sigma_z J_1(\rmM s).
\end{equation}
with $s=\sqrt{\rmc^2\tau^2-\Delta x^2}$ where $\tau=t-t'$ and $\Delta x=x-x'$. The Pauli matrices in the propagator \cref{eq:FeynmanEva_K} assume the following representation of the $1+1$D Dirac equation 
$$
(\rmi\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu-\rmm\rmc/\hbar)\Psi=0
$$
with $\gamma^0=\sigma_x$ and $\gamma^1=-\rmi\sigma_y$. We should also note that, in Feynman's model, all paths are confined inside the light cone which is illustrated as a red line in \cref{fig:FCM}. Thus we should multiply \cref{eq:FeynmanEva_K} with a factor $\theta(s^2)$ with $\theta$ being the Heaviside step function.  As we shall now show, the propagator in \cref{eq:FeynmanEva_K} omits two delta function terms. To find those missing terms, let us apply the method described in the previous sections to the $1+1$D Dirac equation. 

Using the same approach given above, we see the function $F(\Psi,G)$ in \cref{eq:continuity} is,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Dirac_F}
    F(\Psi,G)=\rmi G^\dagger\gamma^\mu\Psi.
\end{equation}
Similarly, we choose the volume $V$ in \cref{eq:Key_Idea} to be the upper hemisphere of the spacetime coordinates, $x$  and $t$ (if $x_A$ is in the upper hemisphere). Since $\partial_\mu(\rmi G^\dagger\gamma^\mu\Psi)=\delta(x_A-x_B)\Psi(x_B)$, we have
\begin{equation*}
    \Psi(x_A)=\int_{t=0} -\rmi G(x_A-x_B)^\dagger\gamma^0\Psi(x_B){\rm d}^3x
\end{equation*}
which shows directly that the propagator for the Dirac equation is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Dirac_propagator}
    K(x_A-x_B)=-\rmi G(x_A-x_B)^\dagger\gamma^0.
\end{equation}
There are four choices of contours for the Green's function in \cref{eq:Dirac_propagator}. Two of them are not restricted in the light cone. In order that paths are confined inside the light cone, we choose the retarded Green's function
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Dirac_G_proto}
    G=-(\rmi\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu+\rmM)G_+
\end{equation}
where $G_+$ is the retarded Klein--Green function in \cref{eq:KGEq_RAGreen}. Explicitly, the $1+1$D retarded Dirac--Green function \cref{eq:Dirac_G_proto} is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Dirac_G}
    \begin{array}{cl}
        G= & -M \theta\left(\tau-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)J_0(\rmM s)
        \\
         & -\sigma_x\frac\rmi\rmc\left[\delta\left(\tau-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)J_0(\rmM s)\right.\\&\left.\quad-\theta\left(\tau-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)\frac{\rmM\rmc^2\tau}sJ_1(\rmM s)\right]
         \\
         &-\sigma_y\left[-\frac1\rmc\delta\left(\tau-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)J_0(\rmM  s){\rm sign}(\Delta x)\right.\\&\left.\quad+\theta\left(\tau-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)\frac{\rmM\Delta x}sJ_1(\rmM s)\right]
    \end{array}
\end{equation}
with $\tau=t_A-t_B$, $s=\sqrt{\rmc^2\tau^2-|\Delta x|^2}$. Therefore the propagator \cref{eq:Dirac_propagator} becomes
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Dirac_K}
    \begin{array}{cl}
         K=&  \rmi\sigma_x \rmM\theta\left(\tau-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)J_0(\rmM s)
         \\
         &-\theta\left(\tau-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)\frac{\rmM\rmc\tau}sJ_1(\rmM s)
         \\
         &-\sigma_z\theta\left(\tau-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)\frac{\rmM\Delta x}sJ_1(\rmM s)
         \\
         & +\frac1\rmc\delta\left(\tau-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right)
         \\
         &+\sigma_z\frac1\rmc\delta\left(\tau-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc\right){\rm sign}(\Delta x).
    \end{array}
\end{equation}
The first three non--delta terms in propagator \cref{eq:Dirac_K} are identical to those in the propagator \cref{eq:FeynmanEva_K} that is derived from the Feynman checkerboard model~\cite{kull1999path}, except for a normalization factor $\rmi \rmM$. The propagator \cref{eq:Dirac_K} becomes a $\delta$ function when $\tau$ vanishes. In contrast, the propagator \cref{eq:FeynmanEva_K} does not. Notably, when the mass vanishes--- a case not considered by Feynman, namely, $\rmm=0$, the first three terms in \cref{eq:Dirac_K} also vanish as does the propagator \cref{eq:FeynmanDef_K}. In this case, the Dirac equation is identical to the $1+1$D Maxwell equations in free space. These $\delta$ terms in the propagator \cref{eq:Dirac_K} simply mean that the wave propagates freely with a retarded time $|\Delta x|/\rmc$. This fact is excluded from Feynman's propagator \cref{eq:FeynmanDef_K}~\cite{FeynmanQM} which vanishes when $\rmm=0$. In fact, the additional $\delta\left(\tau-|\Delta x|/\rmc\right)$ is related to those paths on the light cone which have no corner in the Feynman checker board model, as illustrated as a red line in \cref{fig:FCM}. These discrepancies arise because the Feynman checkerboard model is a discrete model. For paths lying on the light cone, the amplitude is 1, which, in fact, is a $\delta_{\rmc\tau,\Delta x}$ and should be $\delta(\rmc\tau-\Delta x)$ in the continuous limit.


For the $3+1$D Dirac equation, we shall substitute the Green function \cref{eq:KGEq_G_ret} into \cref{eq:Dirac_G_proto} to obtain the $3+1$D Dirac--Green function and then use it to evaluate the Dirac propagator \cref{eq:Dirac_propagator}. Note that the $\gamma$ matrices are now $4\times4$ objects. The $3+1$D Dirac propagator \cref{eq:Dirac_propagator} again differs from the one given in earlier works (e.g. Ref.~\cite{ord2023feynman}), which make use of a $3+1$D Feynman checkerboard model, as evident from the presence of Bessel and delta functions in \cref{eq:KGEq_G_ret}. Furthermore, when $\tau=0$, the propagator should be a delta function whereas the one in Ref.~\cite{ord2023feynman} is 1, which is, in fact, $\delta_{\rmc\tau,\Delta x}$---the same issue discussed above, arising from the discrete nature of the Feynman checkerboard model. We can conclude that the propagator in \cref{eq:Dirac_propagator} is the correct one, as it is derived directly from the wave equation itself without relying on any model.



Now, having introduced the Feynman checkerboard model for the Dirac equation, let us return to our earlier discussion of the $1+1$D Klein--Gordon equation. We can now see that the $1+1$D Klein--Gordon propagator \cref{eq:11KGEq_K}, when neglecting the $\delta$ terms, is equal to the sum of the trace of the time--forward and time--backward $1+1$D
Dirac propagator \cref{eq:FeynmanEva_K}. Therefore, the $1+1$D Klein--Gordon propagator \cref{eq:11KGEq_K} can be interpreted using the Feynman checkerboard model as follows. Consider a particle that moves in one dimension and is only allowed to move forward or backward at the speed of light. If the particle initially moves forward (backward), it must also move forward (backward) at the final time step, namely, the particle must retain its initial state of motion when observed. Moreover, the particle must move both forward and backward in time simultaneously, in a mirrored manner, as illustrated in \cref{fig:KleinFCM}.
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{KleinFCM.jpg}
    \caption{\textbf{The Feynman checkerboard model for the Klein--Gordon equation:} The red line is the projection of the light cone on the right half $x$--$t$ plane. The particle moves both forward and backward in time simultaneously, in a mirror manner. The particle retains its initial direction of motion at the final time step.}
    \label{fig:KleinFCM}
\end{figure}
That is, if the particle moves forward in time to travel from $(x_B,0)$ to $(x_A,t_A)$, it will simultaneously move backward in time to travel from $(x_B,0)$ to $(x_A,-t_A)$ in a mirrored manner. For each path connecting $(x_B,0)$ to $(x_A,\pm t_A)$, the wave function, just like the Dirac equation, carries an amplitude of 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:KGEq_FCM_amp}
    \phi=(\rmi\rmM\epsilon)^R
\end{equation}
with $\epsilon$ the small time step and $R$ the number of corners.  Also, we do not need to distinguish the spin of the particle, since there is no spin information in the Klein--Gordon equation. Therefore, the propagator is simply the sum of $K_{++}$, the propagator for paths that start and end with a forward move, and $K_{--}$, the propagator for paths that start and end with a backward move. Finally, the propagator should be
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Klein_FCM}
K=\underset{R}\sum N(R;t_A)(\rmi \rmM\epsilon)^R+ N(R;-t_A)(\rmi \rmM\epsilon)^R
\end{equation}
where  $N(R;t_A)$ is the total number of paths with $R$ corners from $(x_B,0)$ to $(x_A,t_A)$ while $N(R;-t_A)$ the total number of paths with $R$ corners from $(x_B,0)$ to $(x_A,-t_A)$. The propagator \cref{eq:Klein_FCM} can be evaluated using the method in Ref.~\cite{kull1999path} and is equal to the propagator \cref{eq:11KGEq_K} without those $\delta$ terms. Those $\delta$ terms represent the amplitude of the paths lying on the light cone. However, since the Feynman checkerboard model is discrete, the amplitude of paths on the light cone is given as 1 which is actually $\delta_{\rmc\tau,\Delta x}$ and it fails to be correctly generalized as $\delta(\tau-|\Delta x|/\rmc)$ in the continuous limit, as we discussed above.



\section{Conclusion}
Based on the Rayleigh diffraction integral in spacetime, we find the propagators of both the Klein--Gordon equation and the Dirac equation. Additionally, we explain the propagator of the $1+1$D Klein--Gordon equation using the Feynman checkerboard model, finding that the particle moves forward and backward in time simultaneously in a mirrored manner and must retain its initial direction of motion at the final time step. As for the Feynman checkerboard model, we compare our $1+1$D Dirac propagator~\cref{eq:Dirac_K} with the propagator given by the Feynman checkerboard model~\cite{kull1999path,FeynmanQM} and find an issue that the $\delta$ functions' terms have been neglected in the Feynman checkerboard model. This leads to the defect that its propagator cannot be reduced to a $\delta$ function when the time step vanishes. Moreover, in the case when the mass vanishes, the Feynman's propagator~\cite{kull1999path} also vanishes. This is not correct since those $\delta$ terms are simply related to the free propagation with a retarded time $|\Delta x|/\rmc$. In fact, this problem results from the amplitude being $1$ on the paths on the light cone, which should be $\delta(\tau-\frac{|\Delta x|}\rmc)$ in the continuous limit. We also discuss the $3+1$D Dirac's propagator and find that the $3+1$D propagator derived from the $3+1$D Feynman checkerboard model Ref.~\cite{ord2023feynman} has the same problems.


