\section{Sufficient conditions}\label{sec: sufficient conditions}

We begin by identifying simple sufficient conditions under which a question profile $X$ is incentivizable. In Section \ref{sec: complete}, we show that these conditions are also necessary in some natural applications.

\begin{defn}
Questions $\vt{X}(a)$ and $\vt{Y}(a)$ are \emph{equivalent} if there exist $\gamma(a)\in\R\setminus\{0\}$ and $\kappa(a)\in\R$ such that
\[
\vt{X}\left(a\right)=\gamma (a)\vt{Y}(a)+\kappa (a) \vt{1},
\]
where $\vt{1}\in \R^\Theta$ is the vector of all ones. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are \emph{equivalent} if they are equivalent for all $a\in A$.
%Questions $X$ and $Y$ are \emph{equivalent at $a\in A$} if there exist $\gamma(a)\in\R\setminus\{0\}$ and $\kappa(a)\in\R$ such that
%\[
%\vt{X}\left(a\right)=\gamma (a)\vt{Y}(a)+\kappa (a) \vt{1},
%\]
%where $\vt{1}\in \R^\Theta$ is the vector of all ones. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are \emph{equivalent} if they are equivalent at all $a\in A$.
\end{defn}

Two questions are equivalent %at $a$
if they are invertible affine transformations of one another. %(when evaluated at $a$). 
In particular, for any given belief, knowing the expectation of one of them is sufficient to calculate the expectation of the other. Switching from one question to the other therefore amounts to shifting and rescaling the decision-maker's reports, and does not affect the information the researcher would obtain from truthful responses.

\begin{defn}
A question profile $X$ is \emph{aligned with $u$ on $B\subseteq A$} if there exists $\vt{d}\in\R^\Theta$ such that either $\vt{X}(a)$ is equivalent to the question $\vt{u}\left(a\right)+\vt{d}$ for all $a\in B$, or $\vt{X}(a)$ is equivalent to the question $\vt{d}$ for all $a\in B$. We say that $X$ is \emph{nontrivially aligned with $u$ on $B$} in the former case, and \emph{trivially} so in the latter case. If $B=A$, we say simply that $X$ is \emph{aligned with $u$} (and similarly with the (non)-trivial qualifier).
\end{defn}


