For any vector $\vt{v}\in\R^{\Theta}$, let 
$\bar{\vt{v}}=\vt{v}-\frac{1}{|\Theta|}\sum_{\theta'\in\Theta} v(\theta')\vt{1}$
be the projection of $\vt{v}$ onto the hyperplane of vectors whose coordinates sum to $0$. 
%define $\bar{v}$ so that for each $\theta$, $\bar{v}\left(\theta\right)=v\left(\theta\right)-\frac{1}{\left|\Theta\right|}\sum v\left(\theta^{\prime}\right)$. 
Recall that two vectors $\vt{u},\vt{v}\in\R^\Theta$ are \textit{collinear} if there exists $\alpha\neq0$ such that $\vt{v}=\alpha \vt{u}$.

Let $\Delta_a^b=\bar{\vt{u}}(b)-\bar{\vt{u}}(a)$ be the payoff difference vector. 

\begin{lem}[Adjacency Lemma]\label{lem: adjacency}
Suppose that $X$ is incentivizable. Then it is aligned with $u$ on $\{a,b\}$ for every adjacent pair of actions $a$ and $b$. Equivalently, for each pair of adjacent actions $a$ and $b$, there exist $\rho\in\R$ and $\sigma\in\R\setminus\{0\}$ such that 
\[
\bar{\vt{X}}\left(b\right)=\rho\Delta_a^b+\sigma\bar{\vt{X}}\left(a\right); 
\]
%\[
%\bar{\vt{X}}\left(b\right)=\rho\left(\bar{\vt{u}}(b)-\bar{\vt{u}}(a)\right)+\sigma\bar{\vt{X}}\left(a\right).
%\]
if $\bar{\vt{X}}(a)$ or $\bar{\vt{X}}(b)$ is collinear with $\Delta_a^b$, then we can take $\rho\neq 0$.
\end{lem}

The Adjacency Lemma provides a key tool in testing whether a question in incentivizable: it identifies a restriction on the values of the question on each pair of adjacent actions $a$ and $b$. 
%An equivalent way to state this restriction is that the vector $\bar{\vt{X}}(b)$ must belong to the linear subspace spanned by the vectors $\bar{\vt{X}}(a)$ and $\Delta_a^b$. 

To understand the intuition behind this result, recall first that the expected optimal payoff $\max_{a,r}\E_pV(a,r,\cdot)$ is a convex function of the belief $p$ and is affine over a convex set of beliefs if and only if there is a single optimal choice $(a,r)$ for all beliefs in that set. In particular, if $a$ and $b$ are adjacent actions, for any given $r$, the optimal payoff is affine over the set $S=\{p:a,b\in A(p),r=\E_p X(a,\cdot)\}$. The first condition for incentivizability implies that action $b$ is optimal at all beliefs in the set $S$. Because the optimal payoff is affine over $S$, there must be a unique optimal choice of report $r'$ following $b$. The second condition for incentivizability further implies that the expected value of question $\vt{X}(b)$ is constant (and equal to $r'$) within the set $S$. 
%consider two adjacent actions, $a$ and $b$. Suppose an elicitation method $V$ incentivizes some $X$. Notice that, for any fixed $r$, the expected value $\E_p [V(r,a,\cdot)]$ is affine in $p$. Moreover, the set of beliefs at which a given $r$ is optimal following action $a$ consists of the intersection of the simplex with the hyperplane defined by $r=\E_p[X(a;\cdot)]$. If this set intersects with the set of beliefs at which both $a$ and $b$ are optimal, then the value obtained from action $b$ followed by the optimal report must itself be affine within this intersection. This in turn implies that the optimal report $r'=\E_p[X(b;\cdot)]$ following action $b$ must be constant along this intersection since the value for any fixed report is affine. In other words, for all beliefs such that the DM is indifferent between the two actions and the expected value of the question for action $a$ is constant, the expected value for action $b$ must be constant as well. 
A standard linear algebra argument shows that the vector $\vt{X}(b)$ must belong to the linear space spanned by the vectors $\vt{u}(b)-\vt{u}(a)$, $\vt{X}(a)$, and $\vt{1}$ (the latter because we apply the linear condition to the space of beliefs). The result then follows from straightforward algebra.

Because the projection $\bar{\vt{v}}$ maps vectors $\vt{v}$ into a $|\Theta-1|$-dimensional space, the Adjacency Lemma has no bite when $|\Theta|=2$, and limited bite when $|\Theta|=3$. We discuss this issue in Section \ref{sec: Three states}.

