Given a decision problem $(\Theta,A,u)$, say that two actions $a,b\in A$ are \emph{adjacent} if there is a belief $p\in\Delta (\Theta)$ such that $A(p)=\{a,b\}$, that is, at belief $p$, $a$ and $b$ are both optimal and there is no other optimal action. Adjacency of actions $a$ and $b$ implies that there is a $\left(\left|\Theta\right|-2\right)$-dimensional set of beliefs $p\in\Delta(\Theta)$ such that $a,b\in\arg\max_{c\in A}\E_{p}u\left(c;\cdot\right)$. The \emph{adjacency graph} is the undirected graph with vertices $A$ and edges consisting of the adjacent pairs $\{a,b\}$. Note that, since there are no redundant or dominated actions, the adjacency graph is connected for every decision problem.


