\section{Adjacency trees}\label{sec: trees}

We first consider the case in which the adjacency graph is a tree (i.e., has no cycles). Trees naturally arise in problems with ordered actions, as in Example \ref{ex: second order beliefs}. Example \ref{ex: star} below describes a simple decision problem in which the adjacency graph forms a star. In this case, as the following result shows, alignment with $u$ on the full set of actions is not necessary for incentivizability.

\begin{thm}\label{prop: tree}
Suppose the adjacency graph is a tree. Then $X$ is incentivizable if and only if it is aligned with $u$ on every pair of adjacent actions.
