It must be that $d(\theta)=-u(a,\theta)$ for each $a$ and $\theta$ such that $u(a,\theta)<x$. This would imply that, for each $\theta$, $u(a,\theta)=u(b,\theta)$ for any two actions $a$ and  $b$ such that $u(a,\theta)<x$ and $u(b,\theta)<x$. But this contradicts our assumption, which implies the existence of two different actions, $a$ where all answers are wrong and $b$ where exactly one answer is wrong. 

