To evaluate the speed-up achieved by our CP-decomposition-based tensor product in~\cref{sec:cp}, we benchmark its wall-clock runtime against the fully connected CG tensor product implementation in \texttt{e3nn}. As shown in~\cref{fig:time}, the proposed approximation accelerates by factors of \(4.0\times, 4.8\times, 15.0\times, 26.7\times, 47.6\times,\) and \(83.6\times\) for maximum degrees \(L=1,2,3,4,5,\) and \(6\), respectively.  

Since TDN is derived from Equiformer by replacing each CG block with its CP decomposition and incorporating path-weight sharing, we further evaluate end-to-end throughput and parameter count for both networks under identical irrep configurations across varying \(L\). All experiments are conducted on a single NVIDIA A100-80GB GPU with batch size 128 and identical input irreps. The results in~\cref{tab: throughput} show that TDN processes \(1.23\times\) to \(2.95\times\) more structures per second and uses \(50\%\)–\(87\%\) fewer parameters than Equiformer. As the maximum degree $L$ increases, TDN achieves higher throughput and requires fewer parameters.


\subsection{Results on PubChemQCR}
\label{sec:exp-pubchem}

We evaluate our method on both PubChemQCR‑S and the full PubChemQCR dataset. For the small split we compare against nine state‑of‑the‑art models: SchNet~\citep{schutt2018schnet}, PaiNN~\citep{schutt2021equivariant}, MACE~\citep{batatia2022mace}, PACE~\citep{xu2024equivariant}, FAENet~\citep{duval2023faenet}, NequIP~\citep{batzner20223}, SevenNet~\citep{park2024scalable}, Allegro~\citep{musaelian2023learning}, and Equiformer~\citep{liao2022equiformer}.  
On the full dataset we benchmark against SchNet and PaiNN, the only baselines that scale to its size within our hardware budget. Performance is reported as mean absolute error (MAE) for energies and root‑mean‑square error (RMSE) for forces. The results are shown in~\cref{tb:full}, the proposed TDN model achieves the lowest energy and force prediction errors across PubChemQCR‑S and the full PubChemQCR dataset, significantly outperforming all baseline methods. Moreover, the performance further improves as the dataset size increases.


\subsection{Results on OC20}
