\textbf{Model Configuration}: The proposed integrated GARCH-GRU architecture is implemented by introducing custom modifications to the standard GRU cell, embedding conditional heteroskedasticity dynamics in a manner consistent with the GARCH(1,1) specification. A parallel implementation is applied to the integrated GARCH-LSTM model, serving as a comparative counterpart. This hybrid design enables the models to simultaneously capture nonlinear temporal dependencies and time-varying volatility structures inherent in financial time series.

To optimize model performance, hyperparameter tuning is conducted using the Optuna framework, leveraging a Bayesian optimization strategy for efficient exploration of the parameter space. To prevent overfitting, an early stopping criterion is applied based on validation set performance, with a patience threshold of 10 epochs. The mean squared error (MSE) is used as the monitoring metric. Model training is carried out using the Adam optimizer, which offers adaptive learning rates and robust convergence properties. This training configuration ensures both stability during optimization and strong generalization across varying forecasting horizons, supporting the empirical robustness of the proposed hybrid volatility models.

\textbf{Evaluation Framework}: The forecasting performance of the proposed model is evaluated using two complementary statistical accuracy metrics: Mean Squared Error (MSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE). These metrics are computed over the out-of-sample forecast horizon to quantify the deviation of predicted volatility from realized volatility. The Mean Squared Error is defined as

$$
\mathrm{MSE}:=\frac{1}{n} \sum_{t=1}^n\left(\hat{\sigma}_t-\sigma_t\right)^2
$$
where $\hat{\sigma}_t$ and $\sigma_t$ denote the predicted and realized volatility at time $t$, respectively. MSE penalizes larger forecast errors more heavily due to the form of the quadratic loss function, making it particularly sensitive to extreme deviations.
In contrast, the Mean Absolute Error is calculated as
$$
\mathrm{MAE}:=\frac{1}{n} \sum_{t=1}^n\left|\hat{\sigma}_t-\sigma_t\right|
$$
which provides a more \textit{robust} measure of average forecast accuracy that is less influenced by outliers. Together, MSE and MAE offer a comprehensive view of both the magnitude and variability of forecast errors, facilitating comparative evaluation across benchmark models and robustness checks.

In addition to these statistical loss metrics, we employ Value-at-Risk (VaR) forecasting as a complementary evaluation tool to assess the practical utility of the volatility predictions in risk management. VaR violations and violation ratios are analyzed to quantify the accuracy of the forecasted volatility in capturing downside risk. This dual focus on statistical precision and financial relevance enables a rigorous and well-rounded evaluation of the proposed hybrid model.

\textbf{Benchmarks}: To rigorously evaluate the forecasting performance of the proposed integrated GARCH-GRU model, we compare it against four representative classes of benchmark models that are widely studied in the volatility forecasting literature. These benchmarks are selected to reflect a broad spectrum of modeling paradigms, encompassing classical econometric approaches, standard deep learning architectures, and both embedded and pipeline-based hybrid frameworks.
The first benchmark class includes embedded hybrid models, specifically the architecture proposed in \cite{zhao2024garch}, which modifies the LSTM cell by replacing the output gate with a GARCH-type regression. This embedded integration enables the model to learn both volatility dynamics and sequential dependencies within a unified recurrent structure, facilitating end-to-end training. We refer to this model as `bl\_GARCH\_LSTM', and designate it as the baseline hybrid benchmark, given its architectural similarity to our GARCH-GRU model and its innovative embedding of econometric dynamics within deep learning modules.
The second benchmark class comprises pipeline hybrid models, where the output of a GARCH model, typically the estimated conditional variances, are used as input features for a recurrent neural network. This decoupled, two-stage integration strategy allows the GARCH component to capture short-term volatility clustering, while the LSTM component learns additional nonlinear and long-range dependencies. We denote this model as `pl\_GARCH\_LSTM', representing a common and pragmatic approach in hybrid modeling that leverages the strengths of both statistical and machine learning techniques in a modular fashion.
The third class consists of pure deep learning models, including standard GRU, LSTM, and Transformer architectures. These models are capable of learning rich temporal patterns and nonlinear dependencies, but do not incorporate any explicit econometric mechanisms for modeling volatility. As such, they serve as benchmarks for evaluating the added value of incorporating structured volatility dynamics into neural networks. 
The fourth and final class includes traditional econometric models, specifically GARCH(1,1) and GJR-GARCH(1,1,1). These models are well-established in the literature for capturing conditional heteroskedasticity and leverage effects in financial return series. Their inclusion provides a point of comparison for assessing the performance gains achieved through hybridization with neural networks.
Together, these benchmark classes, comprising classical models, standard deep learning architectures, pipeline hybrids, and embedded hybrids, form a comprehensive evaluation framework. This allows us to position the proposed GARCH-GRU model within a broad methodological context, ensuring that observed performance improvements are both robust and interpretable, and reflect meaningful advances over existing approaches in the literature.

\textbf{Architectural Validation}:
Prior to the construction of the integrated GARCH-GRU architecture, we conducted a rigorous validation of the custom neural network (NN) components to ensure their correctness and reliability. Since the hybrid model relies on manually implemented neural cells to allow GARCH integration at the architectural level, it was essential to verify that these custom components replicate the functional behavior of standard, well-tested NN implementations. This validation step was crucial to attribute any observed performance difference in the hybrid model solely to the intended GARCH-NN fusion, rather than unintended implementation artifacts.
To assess implementation equivalence, we treated the custom and standard neural networks as two deterministic functions, $f_1(x)$ and $f_2(x)$, respectively. A set of randomly generated input vectors $x$ was passed through both networks, yielding output sequences $y_1=f_1(x)$ and $y_2=f_2(x)$. The output were then compared using a paired t-test, which evaluates whether the mean difference between paired observations from the two models is statistically significant.
Formally, we tested the null hypothesis 
$$H_0: \mathbb{E}\left[y_1-y_2\right]=0$$ 
against the alternative 
$$H_1: \mathbb{E}\left[y_1-y_2\right] \neq 0$$

The results of the paired t-test are summarized in Table.\ref{tab:custom model validation}. For both GRU and LSTM architectures, the mean differences between the custom and standard implementations were effectively zero, and the corresponding p-values substantially exceeded conventional significance thresholds (e.g., $\alpha$ = 0.01). These results indicate that there is no statistically significant difference between the output of the custom and standard models.
