In the overall comparison of the model forecasting errors across the three datasets, our integrated GARCH-GRU model consistently outperforms other benchmark models, achieving the lowest MSE and MAE values in most forecasting scenarios. This superior performance is especially evident in medium- and long-term forecasting horizons (3-day and 1-week) across the S\&P 500 and NASDAQ indices. An exception arises in the case of the Dow Jones Industrial Average (DJI) at the 1-day and 3-day forecast horizons, where the GARCH-GRU model exhibits slightly higher forecasting errors than the baseline GRU model. This performance difference can be attributed to several factors. Notably, the DJI consists of a smaller and more stable group of blue-chip stocks, which typically exhibit lower short-term volatility and weaker clustering effects compared to the broader S\&P 500 or the more volatility-prone, technology-driven NASDAQ. In such settings, the benefits of incorporating GARCH-style persistence into the model may be limited, and purely data-driven architectures like GRU may offer better adaptability to short-term fluctuations that are less driven by persistent volatility shocks.
Nonetheless, the differences in errors are relatively small and statistically insignificant, and the integrated GARCH-GRU model still provides a more interpretable and principled framework.

When compared to the integrated GARCH-LSTM model, the GARCH-GRU consistently outperforms across all datasets and forecast horizons. On the S\&P 500, for example, the GARCH-GRU model achieves lower errors at every horizon, with the largest gap observed at the 1-week forecast, where the MSE is reduced from 0.1180 to 0.0785, and MAE is reduced from 0.2534 to 0.2077. Similar improvements are seen on the NASDAQ and Dow Jones (DJI) indices. This performance gap reflects the efficiency of the GRU architecture, which, despite having a more compact gating structure, proves more effective when tightly integrated with GARCH dynamics. The GRU's reduced parameter count may also contribute to better generalization in volatility forecasting tasks, especially under data constraints or high volatility regimes.

Beyond its direct comparison with the GARCH-LSTM model, the proposed GARCH-GRU architecture also outperforms alternative hybrid configurations, including the embedded GARCH-LSTM (bl\_GARCH\_LSTM), which replaces the LSTM output gate with a GARCH regression, and the pipeline-based GARCH-LSTM (pl\_GARCH\_LSTM), which sequentially combines GARCH and neural network components. 
These alternative designs often suffer from limited integration or strong constraints, thereby constraining the model’s ability to fully capture the complex interplay between temporal dynamics and volatility behavior. For instance, on the S\&P 500 index at the 1-day horizon, the pipeline model (pl\_GARCH\_LSTM) yields an MSE of 0.0224, while the embedded model (bl\_GARCH\_LSTM) performs significantly worse with an MSE of 0.0616. In contrast, the GARCH-GRU achieves a lower MSE of 0.0202, demonstrating its superior forecasting precision. These results highlight the advantages of a tightly coupled, end-to-end architecture, where GARCH dynamics are structurally embedded within the GRU’s update equations. Such integration facilitates more expressive representation learning and enables the model to coherently internalize both nonlinear temporal dependencies and heteroskedastic variance patterns, leading to improved volatility forecasts.

The proposed GARCH-GRU model also demonstrates clear advantages over standalone neural network architectures, including standard GRU, LSTM, and Transformer models. Although these models are capable of learning nonlinear temporal patterns, they lack explicit mechanisms for modeling volatility `stylized facts' such as clustering or long memory. The GARCH-GRU model bridges this gap by embedding statistical volatility dynamics into the hidden state transitions. On the Dow Jones (DJI) index at the 1-week horizon, the standalone GRU and LSTM models yield MSEs of 0.0931 and 0.0847, respectively, while the GARCH-GRU model reduces the error to 0.0759. While the Transformer model performs competitively on certain short-term horizons, its performance degrades over longer horizon, suggesting that its attention mechanism lacks the necessary structural bias for volatility modeling. These results underline the value of combining econometric priors with neural architectures to improve robustness and long-horizon predictive power.

In comparison with the traditional econometric models, such as GARCH(1,1) and GJR-GARCH, the GARCH-GRU model achieves substantial improvements across all datasets and forecasting horizons. The classical models, while interpretable and grounded in financial theory, are limited in their capacity to capture nonlinear dependencies and complex temporal structures. Across all datasets and horizons, the GARCH-GRU model achieves large reductions in both MSE and MAE. For example, on the S\&P 500 dataset for 1-day forecasts, GARCH(1,1) and GJR-GARCH models report MSEs of 0.0795 and 0.0863, respectively, compared to 0.0202 from the proposed model, which reaches a reduction of over 70\%. Similar margins can be seen in longer-horizon forecasts and other datasets, underscoring the limitations of purely statistical models in modern financial environments characterized by nonlinear and regime-dependent volatility dynamics.

Furthermore, to assess computational efficiency, we measured the average training time for both the integrated GARCH-GRU and GARCH-LSTM models across 20 runs with different random seeds. The results show that the GARCH-GRU model requires only 133.52 seconds on average, compared to 351.57 seconds for the GARCH-LSTM model. It indicates that the GARCH-GRU model achieves approximately 62\% reduction in training time. This substantial reduction in training time highlights the computational efficiency of the GRU architecture, which benefits from a simpler gating mechanism while preserving strong sequence modeling capabilities. The ability of the GARCH-GRU model to achieve superior forecasting accuracy with significantly lower computational cost further underscores the advantage of integrating GARCH dynamics with GRU. This makes the proposed model particularly appealing for large-scale or real-time financial forecasting applications, where both performance and efficiency are critical.


\section{Applications}
One of the most important practical applications of volatility forecasting lies in financial risk management, particularly in the computation of Value-at-Risk (VaR). VaR is a widely adopted quantitative risk measure that estimates the maximum potential loss of a portfolio or asset over a specified time horizon at a given confidence level. It is extensively used by financial institutions, regulators, and portfolio managers to assess market risk exposure, allocate capital, and comply with regulatory requirements such as those imposed under the Basel Accords.

Formally, for a return series ${r_t}$, the one-period ahead VaR at significance level $\alpha \in (0,1)$ is defined as the quantile of the conditional return distribution such that the probability of a loss exceeding the VaR level is no greater than $\alpha$. Mathematically, the VaR at level $\alpha$, denoted $\text{VaR}_{t+1}^{\alpha}$, satisfies: \begin{equation} 
\mathbb{P}(r_{t+1} < -\text{VaR}_{t+1}^{\alpha} \mid \mathcal{F}_t) = \alpha
\end{equation} where $\mathcal{F}_t$ represents the information set available at time $t$. Under the assumption that the return series have constant mean $\mu$, in this study, rather than assuming a parametric distribution (e.g., Gaussian), we estimate VaR using a non-parametric approach based on the empirical distribution of standardized returns. Specifically, we compute:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{VaR}_{t+1}^\alpha=\mu-q_\alpha(\varepsilon) \cdot \hat{\sigma}_{t+1}
\end{equation}
where $q_\alpha(\varepsilon)$ denotes the $\alpha$-quantile of the empirical distribution of $\varepsilon_{t+1}$, estimated from historical standardized residuals $\left\{\hat{\varepsilon}_t\right\}=\left\{\left(r_t-\mu\right) / \sigma_t\right\}$. And $\hat{\sigma}_{t+1}$ is the one-day-ahead forecasted volatility produced by the model. This method allows the VaR estimate to reflect the empirical distributional features of asset returns, such as skewness and fat tails, without imposing restrictive distributional assumptions.

To evaluate the quality of the VaR forecasts, we compute two diagnostic metrics: the violation count and the violation ratio. A violation occurs when the actual return falls below the estimated VaR threshold:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{I}\left\{r_{t+1}<-\operatorname{VaR}_{t+1}^{(\alpha)}\right\}=1
\end{equation}
indicating an underestimation of the true downside risk. The violation count is the total number of such instances, and the violation ratio is computed as:
\begin{equation}
\text { Violation Ratio }=\frac{\text { Number of Violations }}{\text { Total Number of Forecasts }}
\end{equation}
which is compared to the nominal level $\alpha$ to assess whether the model underestimates or overestimates risk.
