\section{Conclusion and Future Work}

This study contributes to the advancement of financial volatility modeling by proposing a novel hybrid architecture that unifies traditional econometric structure with modern deep learning techniques. Specifically, we introduce an integrated GARCH-GRU model, which structurally embeds the GARCH(1,1) volatility dynamics directly into the recurrent computations of a GRU cell through a dedicated multiplicative gate. This architectural design enables the model to simultaneously capture key volatility stylized facts, such as clustering and persistence, while also learning complex nonlinear temporal dependencies inherent in financial time series.

Empirical evaluations conducted on major financial indices, S\&P 500, Dow Jones Industrial Average, and NASDAQ, demonstrate the superior performance of the proposed framework. The integrated GARCH-GRU model consistently achieves lower forecasting errors (MSE and MAE) compared to a range of baseline models, including standalone neural networks (GRU, LSTM, Transformer), traditional GARCH-family models (GARCH(1,1), GJR-GARCH), and several recently proposed hybrid variants. The model not only offers improved statistical accuracy, but also shows computational efficiency, requiring less than 40\% of the training time needed by its LSTM-based counterpart, making it particularly attractive for high-frequency or large-scale financial applications.

In addition to point forecast accuracy, we demonstrate the practical utility of the GARCH-GRU framework in risk management applications through Value-at-Risk (VaR) forecasting. The model produces the lowest violation ratio among all candidates in a one-day-ahead VaR backtesting experiment, aligning closely with the nominal risk level and outperforming both traditional and hybrid benchmarks. This result affirms the model's ability to deliver reliable tail risk estimates and highlights its relevance for real-world deployment in regulatory or capital allocation settings.

Overall, the proposed integrated GARCH-GRU model demonstrates how a tightly coupled, end-to-end integration of econometric structure and neural network architecture can lead to substantial improvements in both forecasting accuracy and model interpretability. It advances beyond conventional hybrid approaches that treat econometric and machine learning components in isolation, offering a unified framework in which both are optimized jointly. This design enables the model to harness the complementary strengths of both paradigms, combining the statistical rigor of GARCH processes with the nonlinear modeling flexibility of deep learning, thereby yielding a more powerful and principled approach to volatility forecasting.

One current limitation of the proposed framework lies in the computational inefficiency introduced by the use of custom recurrent cell implementations. Although these customized cells have been functionally validated and the GARCH-GRU model demonstrates superior efficiency relative to the GARCH-LSTM, requiring significantly less training time, the reliance on non-native deep learning components incurs runtime overhead compared to standard, highly optimized neural modules. This gap becomes more pronounced in large-scale or low-latency applications. To address this, future work will focus on improving computational efficiency through code-level optimizations, operator fusion techniques, and hardware-aware implementation strategies, enabling the model to scale more effectively while retaining its modeling advantages.

Looking ahead, the flexibility of the proposed framework opens several promising directions for future research. One natural extension involves coupling the GRU architecture with asymmetric GARCH variants, such as EGARCH or GJR-GARCH, to better capture leverage effects and asymmetries in volatility responses to positive and negative shocks. Another avenue lies in the integration of attention mechanisms, which could enhance the model’s ability to focus on relevant historical patterns, particularly in high-frequency financial forecasting where data are noisy and regime-dependent. In addition, the framework could be extended to incorporate regime-switching dynamics, allowing it to model structural breaks and transitions between different volatility regimes over time. These enhancements would further deepen the integration of econometric rigor and machine learning flexibility, resulting in models that are not only highly interpretable and scalable, but also robust to the complexities of modern financial markets.


