For the simulation study, we use the fair CRPS as scoring rule. It accounts for the finite number of predictions and estimates the CRPS one would obtain with probabilities estimated from an infinite sample. We consider as target waves with wavenumber $k_t$. 
Then, for a set of wavenumbers $k$ we consider predictions with an ensemble size of $M=8$. The scale-unaware loss and the 3-scale loss are computed for each wavenumber. This is repeated for 4000 realisations of the truth and the predictions. 

Figure~\ref{fig:loss1d} shows that the scale-unaware loss is indeed invariant of the wavenumber of the prediction. In contrast, the 3-scale loss  correctly identifies the target wavenumber.
