\section{Validity of relativistic freeze-in abundance approach}
\label{appendix}




A population of axions with mass $m_a$, produced by freeze-in from the interaction with a thermal bath at temperature $T_{\rm reh}$, has number density
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:neq}
    n_{\rm{eq}}=\frac{1}{2\pi^2}\int_0^\infty \frac{p^2}{e^{\sqrt{p^2+m_a^2}/T_{\rm{reh}}}-1}\,{\rm d}p\,.
\end{equation}
This number density enters the definition of the DM fraction in Eq.~\eqref{eq:primakoff}. In the relativistic limit $m_a\ll T_{\rm reh}$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:primakoff} can be approximated as
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:neq_approx}
    n_{\rm{eq}} \approx \frac{\xi(3)}{\pi^2}\,T_{\rm{reh}}^3\,,
\end{equation}
which leads to the simplified expression in Eq.~\eqref{eq:primakoff_approx}, used throughout our analysis. In this appendix, we compare the freeze-in abundance results shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:dtaumapTRH5MeV} to those obtained using the complete, un-approximated, number density in Eq.~\eqref{eq:neq}. The results, shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:full_tau_contours}, indicate that the two methods lead to consistent constraints, with the full treatment (white solid line) resulting in slightly more conservative bounds than the approximation used in the main text (yellow solid line). Notably, the full treatments present a cutoff in the mass range around $m_a \sim T_{\rm reh}$, which in the exact case arises from the exponential suppression in the phase-space distribution. Since the approximate expression reproduces this cutoff in the constrained value of $(m_a,g_{a\gamma\gamma})$ sufficiently accurately, its use is justified in our study with large ensembles due to the reduced computational cost. Consequently, we do not expect the constraint to be valid for axion masses significantly above $T_{\rm reh}$, contrary to what is reported in Ref.~\cite{Langhoff:2022bij}, see the blue contours in Fig.~\ref{fig:full_tau_contours}.

