\textbf{Running time and complexity} \
The model we proposed has 89.01M parameters, and compressing a RGB-IR image pair of size 1280x1024 requires 2.3GB of GPU memory. The FLOPs of the CCEM module reach 2.85 Mil/pixel. The model we proposed does not focus on real-time performance, but rather emphasizes Rate-Distortion (RD) performance. When tested on both datasets using a single NVIDIA 4090 machine, our model's average encoding time for an RGB-IR image pair is 881ms, and the average decoding time is 942ms. In comparison, prior sequential approaches, such as CVPR2022\cite{b13}, reach an encoding time of 697ms and a decoding time of 601ms. Anchor-based methods, like MLIC++\cite{b10}, reach an encoding time of 901ms and a decoding time of 978ms. While our method introduces slightly more running time costs, it achieves a significant improvement in RD performance. 

