Based on this theoretical relationship (eq.~\ref{eq:nvector_equality}), 
\citet{shimizu2021construction} have reconstructed $\Gamma$ from the potency ${\bf a}$ parametrized along a reference plane. 
The following equation is useful to transform the $n$-vector field to the surface; 
eq.~(\ref{eq:defofnvector}) describes the infinitesimal in $z$ attending the infinitesimal in $\alpha=x,y$,
\begin{equation}
    z_{,\alpha}=-n_\alpha/n_z,
    \label{eq:elevationgradientvsnormal}
\end{equation}
where the subscript following a comma denotes the partial derivative. 
The elevation $z$ is a single-valued function of $x$ and $y$ since we have limited the discussion to the case where the $n$-vector lies within 45 degrees from $n^\circ$ for guaranteeing the unique conversion from potency tensors to $n$-vectors (\S\ref{subsec:forward}).

Equation~(\ref{eq:elevationgradientvsnormal}) states that the cotangent of the $n$-vector coincides with the downward gradient of a curve in a two-dimensional space, and the slip direction perpendicular to the $n$-vector is exactly the fault orientation in two dimensions. 
Hence, \citet{shimizu2021construction} averaged the potency along the short side of the reference surface and smoothly connected the resulting $n$-vector along the long side to extract two-dimensional geometrical features of a uniaxially bended fault (e.g., along-strike strike angle variations and along-dip dip angle variations). 
The same procedure could generate quasi-two-dimensional shapes including uniaxial twists such as along-strike dip variations and along-dip strike variations (\S\ref{subsec:synthetic3}). This paper counts such quasi-two-dimensional shape reconstruction as the previous method by \citet{shimizu2021construction}. 


However, three-dimensional surface reconstruction is beyond the scope of the method of \citet{shimizu2021construction}, which is ascribed to the difference in the degrees of freedom of $n$-vectors between two and three dimensions. 
In two dimensions, the $n$-vector may be expressed by the polar angle $\theta$. 
The transformation of a given $n$-vector field to an elevation $z$ field is then a one-to-one mapping $\theta\mapsto z$, which provides a unique $z$ field to an arbitrary smooth $n$ field. 
By contrast, in three dimensions, 
the $n$-vector may be expressed by the polar angle $\theta$ and the azimuthal angle $\phi$. 
Three-dimensional surface reconstruction from the $n$-vector is therefore a two-to-one mapping $(\theta,\phi)\mapsto z$ from a vector field of $\theta$ and $\phi$ to a scalar field of $z$, generally having no solutions for eqs.~(\ref{eq:nvector_equality}) and (\ref{eq:elevationgradientvsnormal}) unless the given $n$-vector field is error-free. 
Namely, the surface reconstruction from potency is an overdetermined problem in three dimensions. 

Its inverse, the one-to-two mapping of $z$ on $(\theta,\phi)$, is precisely the definition of the normal vector simplified as eq.~(\ref{eq:elevationgradientvsnormal}), 
and it is made bijective by the smoothness of the surface. 
A smooth surface with no tears, the aforementioned regular surface, sets a closed-path integral of relative elevation to zero: 
\begin{equation}
    \oint d{\bf l}\cdot \nabla z=0,
\end{equation}
where $\nabla=(\partial_x,\partial_y)$ denotes the two-dimensional partial differential operator. 
From Stokes' theorem, it is expressed as
\begin{equation}
    \int d\Sigma \nabla\times \nabla z=0,
\end{equation}
that is,
\begin{equation}
    \partial_x\partial_y z=\partial_y\partial_x z.
    \label{eq:commutability}
\end{equation}
Under this constraint on $z$ by eq.~(\ref{eq:commutability}), the one-to-two mapping of $z$ on $(\theta,\phi)$ becomes bijective.
Equation~(\ref{eq:commutability}) indicates that 
the requirement of the smooth surface with no tears is that $\nabla z$ is integrable, that is, the elevation $z$ is a continuous function with no jumps.
Quasi-two-dimensional surface reconstruction is an approximation that neglects that the slope along the long side may vary along the short side (e.g. $\partial_y z_{,x}\simeq 0$), 
so it violates eq.~(\ref{eq:commutability}) when the slope along the short side varies along the long side (e.g. $\partial_x z_{,y}\neq 0$). 

Substituting eq.~(\ref{eq:elevationgradientvsnormal}) into eq.~(\ref{eq:commutability}) leads to 
\begin{equation}
    \partial_x(n_y/n_z)=
    \partial_y(n_x/n_z).
    \label{eq:commutability_constraint_nvect}
\end{equation}
Two-dimensional shapes of uniaxial bending (single-curved surfaces) treated in \citet{shimizu2021construction} always set both sides of eq.~(\ref{eq:commutability_constraint_nvect}) equally to zero. 
However, only the error-free $n$-vector field of a surface meets eq.~(\ref{eq:commutability_constraint_nvect}) in three dimensions. 
Other than such special cases, the estimated normal vectors of curved surfaces violate the integrability condition of eq.~(\ref{eq:commutability_constraint_nvect}), and that is the reason why the $(\theta,\phi)\mapsto z$ mapping of eqs.~(\ref{eq:nvector_equality}) and (\ref{eq:elevationgradientvsnormal}) is overdetermined: 
\begin{equation}
    \partial_x(n_{*y}/n_{*z})\neq
    \partial_y(n_{*x}/n_{*z}).
    \label{eq:noncommutabilitynstar}
\end{equation}
%式(\ref{eq:commutability})を所与の$n$-vectorが満たす特別な状況を除いて、所与の$n$-vectorから標高場$z$への変換は過剰決定である。


