Since the source surface reconstruction via eqs.~(\ref{eq:nvector_equality}) and (\ref{eq:elevationgradientvsnormal}) is overdetermined and eq.~(\ref{eq:elevationgradientvsnormal}) is the identity of the normal vector of a surface, surface reconstruction from given potency must read
eq.~(\ref{eq:nvector_equality}) as some projection:
\begin{equation}
    n(\boldsymbol\xi;\Gamma)\xrightarrow{\mathcal P} n_*(\boldsymbol\xi;{\bf a}),
    \label{eq:nvector_limitequality}
\end{equation}
where $\xrightarrow{\mathcal P}$ denotes some operation to remove non-integrable components such that $\partial_x(n_{*y}/n_{*z})\neq \partial_y(n_{*x}/n_{*z})$. 
Only if that non-integrable portion of $n_*$ is removed, that is, only if illogical tears are ruled out, 
a fault is reconstructed as a smooth surface in a coarse-grained continuum.

Because $n\mapsto z$ mapping is impossible when the theoretical relationship between potency and shape of eq.~(\ref{eq:nvector_equality}) strictly holds, 
surface reconstruction from potency must loosen this premise. There remains, however, arbitrariness in eq.~(\ref{eq:nvector_limitequality}) on how that loosening is applied. We describe this subjective postulation as prior information. 
Equation~(\ref{eq:nvector_limitequality}) expects that the $n$-vector ${\bf n}(\boldsymbol\xi,\Gamma)$ of a surface $\Gamma$ approaches the $n$-vector ${\bf n}_*({\bf a})$ (defined by eq.~\ref{eq:defofnvector_PDTI}) pointed by the nodal planes of potency beachballs. 
The constraint of approximating a normalized vector to a designated value yields a non-Gaussian central-limit distribution known as the von Mises-Fisher distribution; it measures the distance of vectors by the inner product, and for a uniform isotropic case,
\begin{equation}
    P(\Gamma|{\bf a};\kappa)=\exp[\kappa\int_\Gamma d\Sigma(\boldsymbol\xi) {\bf n}_*(\boldsymbol\xi,{\bf a})\cdot {\bf n}(\boldsymbol\xi,\Gamma)]/\mathcal Z,
    \label{eq:prior_Gamma}
\end{equation}
where $\kappa$ denotes a scale factor, and $\mathcal Z$ is a normalization constant. 
The domain of integration is set to $\Gamma$, and $P(\Gamma|{\bf a};\kappa)$ is independent of the way to take the reference surface $\Gamma^\circ$. 

Then, eq.~(\ref{eq:nvector_limitequality}) requires the limit that places ${\bf n}$ in the integrable nearest neighbor of non-integrable ${\bf n}_*$, 
that is, the displacement discontinuity that best approximates given inelastic strain. 
It is termed dislocation limit hereafter, which is, in eq.~(\ref{eq:prior_Gamma}), 
\begin{equation}
    \kappa\to\infty.
\end{equation}
In this limit,
\begin{equation}
    P(\Gamma|{\bf a})=\delta(\Gamma-\Gamma_*({\bf a})),
    \label{eq:prior_Gamma_limit}
\end{equation}
where $\delta(\cdot)$ denotes Dirac delta and 
\begin{equation}
    \Gamma_*:={\rm argmax}_{\Gamma|{\bf a}}P(\Gamma|{\bf a};\kappa).
    \label{eq:defofGammastar}
\end{equation}
Here ${\rm argmax}$ denotes the arguments of the maxima. 
In this paper, $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ is taken as $\lim_{\kappa\to\infty}P(\Gamma|{\bf a},\kappa)$. 


When $(\partial_x\partial_y -\partial_y\partial_x )z$ expected from $n_*$ is small, $n$ obtained from eq.~(\ref{eq:nvector_limitequality}) is not so shifted from $n_*$. 
Basically, we suppose such cases 
and proceed by assuming $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ of eq.~(\ref{eq:prior_Gamma}). 
However, the overdetermination of surface reconstruction, in which any surface cannot obey the forward model eq.~(\ref{eq:defofnvector_PDTI}) that yields eq.~(\ref{eq:nvector_equality}), leaves open the question of what priors $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ are appropriate
to loosen eq.~(\ref{eq:defofnvector_PDTI}) when $(\partial_x\partial_y -\partial_y\partial_x )z$ of $n_*$ is not small. 
Even before the $n$-vector is disturbed by measurement errors that do not satisfy eq.~(\ref{eq:nvector_equality}), 
the fault face as an earthquake source is an approximation of a fracture zone, which accounts for the nature of brittle failure that localizes within a quasi-face~\citep{matsu2019physical}, 
so the exact observance of eq.~(\ref{eq:defofnvector_PDTI}) is originally unphysical. 
Thus, if read as a forward model prior to the observation, $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ for returning a surface from a given $n$-vector field is a probabilistic delineation of the ambiguity of an earthquake fault, an idealization of a thin sheet of inelastic deformation. 
$P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ is different from the posterior probability of fault shape. The $n$-vector-geometry mapping, and therefore the surface reconstruction of earthquake faults from potency, are never beyond Bayesian probabilistic even when potency is given deterministically, 
and the posterior of fault shape is the convolution between the conditional prior of fault shape given potency $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ and the posterior of potency (Appendix \ref{app:A}). 

%一方で、式(\ref{eq:defofGammastar})では最も単純な例として空間一様なvon Mises-Fisher分布を導入したが、これはポテンシー場を形状に翻訳する唯一の確率関数ではない。そもそも純粋な面として断層が存在していない以上、非弾性歪のシートをいかに変位不連続面に射影するべきかの先験的拘束$P(\Gamma| {\bf a})$自体に真値などないのかもしれない。








\section{An analytic solution for $\Gamma_*$ as the optimal elevation field}

It has been shown inevitably probabilistic to project two degrees of freedom in the expected normal $n_*$ onto one degree of freedom in the smooth surface elevation. 
Bearing $P (\Gamma|{\bf a})$ of eq.~(\ref{eq:prior_Gamma}) in mind, 
we derive an analytical expression of the most probable surface estimate $\Gamma_*({\bf a})$ given the potency ${\bf a}$. 
This section assumes that the non-integrable component of $n_*$ (eq.~\ref{eq:noncommutabilitynstar}) is small so that the solution of surface reconstruction does not depend much on the functional form of $P (\Gamma|{\bf a})$.


We have supposed that the potency density ${\bf a}$ uniquely determines the field of the normal vector $n_*$ (\S\ref{subsec:forward}), so the goal of this section is to obtain $\Gamma_*$ given $n_*$. When 
the $x$-$y$ plane describes the location along the reference surface $\Gamma^\circ$, $\Gamma$ is parametrized by $z(\cdot,\cdot)$, which represents the elevation of $\Gamma$ from $\Gamma^\circ$ as a function of the position along the reference surface $(x, y)$.




\subsection{Governing equation}
The governing equation of the problem is now the definitional identity of the $n$-vector (eq.~\ref{eq:elevationgradientvsnormal}). 
It indicates the orthogonality between the $n$-vector and a curved surface, 
which in three dimensions means more than that a 90-degree rotation (cotangent) of the $n$-vector gives the downward surface slope; introducing
\begin{flalign}
    n_\perp&=\sqrt{n_x^2+n_y^2}
    \\
    {\bf n}_\parallel&=(n_x,n_y)/n_\perp,
\end{flalign}
we can rewrite eq.~(\ref{eq:elevationgradientvsnormal}) as
\begin{equation}
    \nabla z = - \frac{n_\perp}{n_z}{\bf n}_\parallel.
\label{eq:elevationgradientvsnormal_meaning}
\end{equation}
Namely, the $n$-vector indicates the direction of the slope by its horizontal projection ${\bf n}_\parallel$ ($|{\bf n}_\parallel|=1$) as well as the downward slope by its horizontal-vertical ratio $n_\perp/n_z$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:4}). 


