The reconstructed shape seems more stable than the reconstructed uniaxial twist. 
This difference suggests that when the $n$-vector noise is at the same level, short-wavelength features such as topography are relatively susceptible to the $n$-vector noise. 
Considering that wider-regional elevations are controlled by longer-wavelength slopes, 
we ascribe this tendency to the spatial averaging that low-passes the slope error in a long-wavelength scale. 
The higher surface reproducibility at longer wavelengths might be reinforced in the application to the PDTI, to which the spatial potency smoothing is commonly applied.

