\section{Estimation error propagation from potency to fault shape}
\label{app:A}
\renewcommand{\theequation}{\thesection.\arabic{equation}}
\setcounter{equation}{0}
The prior of shape $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ shown in \S\ref{sec:Gammaprior} and the posterior of potency $P({\bf a}|{\bf d})$ [$P({\bf a}|{\bf d};\boldsymbol\sigma^2,\boldsymbol\rho^2)$ in the text] set the marginal posterior of fault shape: 
\begin{equation}
    P(\Gamma|{\bf d})=\int d{\bf a}P(\Gamma|{\bf a})P({\bf a}|{\bf d}).
\end{equation}
Here we show the procedure to translate $P({\bf a}|{\bf d})$ into $P(\Gamma|{\bf d})$ when $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ satisfies $n\xrightarrow{\mathcal P} n_*$ (eq.~\ref{eq:nvector_limitequality}). 

For brevity, we introduce the (marginal) posterior of the $n$-vector field $\{{\bf n_*}\}$ associated with the potency density: 
\begin{equation}
    P(\{{\bf n_*}\}|{\bf d})=\int d{\bf a}P({\bf a}|{\bf d})\int d\boldsymbol\xi\delta ({\bf n}_*(\boldsymbol\xi)-{\bf n}_*(\boldsymbol\xi;{\bf a})).
\end{equation}
$P(\{{\bf n_*}\}|{\bf d})$ and $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ of eq.~(\ref{eq:prior_Gamma}) rewrite the marginal posterior of the fault shape as
\begin{equation}
    P(\Gamma|{\bf d})=\int d\{{\bf n_*}\} P(\{{\bf n_*}\}|{\bf d})\exp[\kappa\int d\boldsymbol\xi {\bf n}\cdot{\bf n_*}]/\mathcal Z,
\end{equation}
the right-hand side of which describes the transformation from
the posterior $P(\{{\bf n_*}\}|{\bf d})$ of the fault normal $\{{\bf n}_*\}$ anticipated from potency into 
the posterior $P(\Gamma|{\bf d})$ of fault shape. 
Especially when $\kappa\to\infty$, 
$\exp[\kappa\int d\boldsymbol\xi {\bf n}\cdot{\bf n_*}]$ is extremely sharper than $P(\{{\bf n_*}\}|{\bf d})$ and satisfies $n\xrightarrow{\mathcal P} n_*$ (eq.~\ref{eq:nvector_limitequality}), leading to 
\begin{equation}
    P(\Gamma|{\bf d})=c\int d\{{\bf n_*}\} P(\{{\bf n_*}\}|{\bf d})\int d\boldsymbol\xi \delta ({\bf n}(\boldsymbol\xi)-{\bf n}_*(\boldsymbol\xi)),
    \label{eq:priorofGamma_usingnvect1}
\end{equation}
where $c$ denotes normalization. 
Contrary to its appearance, 
eq.~(\ref{eq:priorofGamma_usingnvect1}) does not conclude that 
the posterior $P(\Gamma|{\bf d})$ of fault surface $\Gamma$ equals 
the posterior $P(\{{\bf n_*}\}|{\bf d})$ of the potency-conforming $n$-vector $n_*$: 
$
    P(\Gamma|{\bf d})\neq P(\{{\bf n_*}\}|{\bf d})|_{{\bf n}_*={\bf n}}.
$
The $n_*$-vector field determined from potency can exceed the definition domain of the $n$-vector field on a smooth surface $\Gamma$ bounded by the surface slope integrability (eq.~\ref{eq:commutability_constraint_nvect}) as indicated in \S\ref{sec:Gammaprior}, 
and therefore eq.~(\ref{eq:priorofGamma_usingnvect1}) becomes
\begin{equation}
    P(\Gamma|{\bf d})= cP(\{{\bf n_*}\}|{\bf d})|_{{\bf n}_*={\bf n}\cap \partial_x(n_y/n_z)=\partial_y(n_x/n_z)}.
    \label{eq:posteriorofshape}
\end{equation}
Thus, searching for the optimum $n$-vector field of a smooth surface from the posterior of potency is a conditional optimization constrained by $\partial_x(n_y/n_z)=\partial_y(n_x/n_z)$. The inference of the optimum elevation automatically satisfies $\partial_x(n_y/n_z)=\partial_y(n_x/n_z)$ and evades this complication as in \S\ref{sec:Gammaprior}. 
The surface reconstruction from this $P(\Gamma|{\bf d})$ is generally a non-linear analysis based on eq.~(\ref{eq:nvectorfromslopes}) relating surface normal ${\bf n}$ and surface slope $\nabla z$. 
The evaluation of eq.~(\ref{eq:nvectorfromslopes}) passes through the similar procedure to that shown in \S\ref{subsec:solvingSPDE_elevation}.
Equation~(\ref{eq:posteriorofshape}) also applies to
$P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ other than eq.~(\ref{eq:prior_Gamma}) 
when $n\xrightarrow{\mathcal P} n_*$ (eq.~\ref{eq:nvector_limitequality}) is met.

We must remark that the non-uniqueness of functional forms of $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$, which was an issue in surface reconstruction from given potency, does not matter in surface reconstruction from the posterior probability of potency $P(\Gamma|{\bf d})$ (eq.~\ref{eq:posteriorofshape}). %Considering the slip inversion evaluate which is the evaluation function of the fault geometry if the source inversion is conducted as in the conventional slip inversion (\S\ref{sec:probabilisticShimizu2021}). 
It could answer why the overdetermination of surface reconstruction from potency has not posed an issue in slip inversions on a movable fault surface. 
After all, we must embrace either the subjectivity inherent in projecting observed inelastic strains onto displacement discontinuities or the subjectivity that forbids all but displacement discontinuities: $P({\bf a},\Gamma|{\bf d})=P({\bf a}|{\bf d})P(\Gamma|{\bf a},{\bf d})\to P({\bf a}|{\bf d})P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ in the text or $P({\bf a},\Gamma|{\bf d})=P({\bf a}|\Gamma,{\bf d})P(\Gamma|{\bf d})$ equivalent to the slip inversions with a movable fault surface (\S\ref{sec:probabilisticShimizu2021}).

\section{The exact solution of $\Gamma_*$ for finite \lowercase{$(\partial_x\partial_y-\partial_y\partial_x)z_*$}}
\label{app:B}
\renewcommand{\theequation}{\thesection.\arabic{equation}}
\setcounter{equation}{0}

The evaluation of $\Gamma_*$ in the text (\S\ref{subsec:solvingSPDE_elevation}) has assumed that the non-integrable component $(\partial_x\partial_y-\partial_y\partial_x)z_*$ expected from the $n$-vector $n_*$ of potency is small. 
Its motive is to avoid an issue of $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ selection arising from finite $(\partial_x\partial_y-\partial_y\partial_x)z_*$
(\S\ref{sec:Gammaprior}, the last paragraph). 
For deeper considerations, we show below the way to evaluate $\Gamma_*$ for given $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ for 
finite $(\partial_x\partial_y-\partial_y\partial_x)z_*$. 
We treat eq.~(\ref{eq:prior_Gamma}) with $\kappa\to\infty$ as a concrete example, and similar results follow other forms of $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$.

Even in the dislocation limit 
$n\xrightarrow{\mathcal P} n_*$ (eq.~\ref{eq:nvector_limitequality}, $\kappa\to\infty$ for eq.~\ref{eq:prior_Gamma}), the $n$-vector of a surface is necessarily deviated from the expectation $n_*$ of potency due to the non-integrable components of $(\partial_x\partial_y-\partial_y\partial_x)z_*$. 
What is expected in this limit is the smallness of the fluctuations from $n_*$ with non-integrable components removed, that is, the $n$-vector of $\Gamma_*$ to be sought itself.
This $n$-vector of $\Gamma_*$, the solution to be sought, is here denoted by $n_*^\prime$: 
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{\kappa\to\infty}{\bf n}(\boldsymbol\xi)={\bf n}_*^\prime(\boldsymbol\xi;\Gamma).
\end{equation}
Therefore, if $(\partial_x\partial_y-\partial_y\partial_x)z_*$ is finite, the fast convergent series in the dislocation limit is the Taylor series of ${\bf n}$ around ${\bf n}_*^\prime$, not the Taylor series of ${\bf n}$ around ${\bf n}_*$ conducted in the text.

We now perform this corrected series expansion to account for $(\partial_x\partial_y-\partial_y\partial_x)z_*$ components dropped in \S\ref{subsec:solvingSPDE_elevation}. 
By utilizing 
\begin{equation}
\left(
    \begin{array}{c}
         \frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}  \\
          \frac{\partial}{\partial\phi}
    \end{array}
    \right)
    \left(
    {\bf n}\,\,{\bf e}_\theta\,\,{\bf e}_\phi
    \right)=
    \left(
    \begin{array}{ccc}
         {\bf e}_\theta& -{\bf n} & {\bf 0}\\
         {\bf e}_\phi\sin\theta& {\bf e}_\phi\cos\theta &-{\bf n}\sin\theta-{\bf e}_\theta\cos\theta
    \end{array}
    \right),
\end{equation}
we obtain the second-order representation of ${\bf n}$ around ${\bf n}_*^\prime$ with respect to the angle fluctuations $\delta\Omega^\prime$ from ${\bf n}_*^\prime$: 
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}    
    {\bf n}=\left[1-\frac 1 2 (\theta-\theta_*^\prime)^2-\frac{\sin^2\theta_*^\prime}2(\phi-\phi_*^\prime)^2\right]{\bf n}_*^\prime
    \\
    +\left[(\theta-\theta_*^\prime)-\frac  12 \sin\theta_*^\prime\cos\theta_*^\prime(\phi-\phi_*^\prime)^2\right]{\bf e}_\theta^{*\prime}
    \\
    +\left[\sin\theta_*^\prime(\phi-\phi*^\prime)+\cos\theta_*^\prime(\theta-\theta_*^\prime)(\phi-\phi_*^\prime)\right]{\bf e}_\phi^{*\prime},
    \end{aligned}
    \label{eq:n_exactseries}
\end{equation}
where $(\theta_*^\prime,\phi_*^\prime)$ denotes the position of ${\bf n}_*^\prime$ on a unit sphere, 
and ${\bf e}_\theta^{*\prime}$ and ${\bf e}_\phi^{*\prime}$ represent associated basis vectors; higher orders become infinitesimally smaller in the dislocation limit. 
Especially for 
$P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ of eq.~(\ref{eq:prior_Gamma}) with $\kappa\to\infty$, 
\begin{flalign}
    {\bf n}_*\cdot{\bf n}=&{\bf n}_*^\prime\cdot{\bf n}-({\bf n}_*^\prime-{\bf n}_*)\cdot{\bf n}
    \\
    =&\left[1-\frac 1 2 (\theta-\theta_*^\prime)^2-\frac{\sin^2\theta_*^\prime}2(\phi-\phi_*^\prime)^2\right]-({\bf n}_*^\prime-{\bf n}_*)\cdot{\bf n}.
\end{flalign}
The first term replicates the right-hand side of eq.~(\ref{eq:nnstarinnerproduct}) with $(\theta_*,\phi_*)$ substituted with $(\theta_*^\prime,\phi_*^\prime)$ and thus functions similarly to those seen in the text. 
The second term is the correction arising from the deviation of ${\bf n}_*^\prime$ from ${\bf n}_*$, explicitly written as
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}    
    ({\bf n}_*^\prime-{\bf n}_*)\cdot{\bf n}=\left[1-\frac 1 2 (\theta-\theta_*^\prime)^2-\frac{\sin^2\theta_*^\prime}2(\phi-\phi_*^\prime)^2\right](1-{\bf n}_*\cdot{\bf n}_*^\prime)
    \\
    -\left[(\theta-\theta_*^\prime)-\frac  12 \sin\theta_*^\prime\cos\theta_*^\prime(\phi-\phi_*^\prime)^2\right]({\bf n}_*\cdot{\bf e}_\theta^{*\prime})
    \\
    -\left[\sin\theta_*^\prime(\phi-\phi*^\prime)+\cos\theta_*^\prime(\theta-\theta_*^\prime)(\phi-\phi_*^\prime)\right]({\bf n}_*\cdot{\bf e}_\phi^{*\prime}).
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Angle fluctuations $\theta-\theta_*^\prime$ and $\phi-\phi_*^\prime$ are expressed by eqs.~(\ref{eq:deltatheta}) and (\ref{eq:deltaphi}) in the text with a substitution $(\theta_*,\phi_*)\to(\theta_*^\prime,\phi_*^\prime)$:
\begin{flalign}
    \theta-\theta_*^\prime&= -n_{*z}^{\prime2}( n_{*\parallel x}^\prime z_{,x}+n_{*\parallel y}^\prime z_{,y}+n_{*\perp}^\prime/n_{*z}^\prime)
    \label{eq:thetaflucutaion_exact}
    \\
    \phi-\phi_*^\prime&= -n_{*\perp}^{\prime-1} n_{*z}^\prime(n_{*\parallel x}^\prime z_{,y}- n_{*\parallel y}^\prime z_{,y}). 
    \label{eq:phiflucutaion_exact}
\end{flalign}
The rest is unaltered from the text, and the final result is: 
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
    \Gamma_*={\rm argmin}
    \int d\Sigma\{
    [(\theta-\theta_*^\prime)^2+\sin^2\theta_*^\prime(\phi-\phi_*^\prime)^2]
    ({\bf n}_*\cdot{\bf n}_*^\prime)
    \\+[-2(\theta-\theta_*^\prime)+ \sin\theta_*^\prime\cos\theta_*^\prime(\phi-\phi_*^\prime)^2]({\bf n}_*\cdot{\bf e}_\theta^{*\prime})
    \\
    -2[\sin\theta_*^\prime(\phi-\phi*^\prime)+\cos\theta_*^\prime(\theta-\theta_*^\prime)(\phi-\phi_*^\prime)]({\bf n}_*\cdot{\bf e}_\phi^{*\prime})\}.
    \end{aligned}
    \label{eq:Gammastarcorrected}
\end{equation}
Equation~(\ref{eq:Gammastarcorrected}) is a self-consistent equation, where the condition to determine $\Gamma_*$ contains 
the $n$-vector of $\Gamma_*$ (${\bf n}_*^\prime$) and the basis vectors
(${\bf e}_\theta^{*\prime}$ and ${\bf e}_\phi^{*\prime}$) at the spherical position $(\theta_*^\prime,\phi_*^\prime)$ of that $n$-vector. 
For small $(\partial_x\partial_y-\partial_y\partial_x)z_*$, 
${\bf n}_*^\prime \simeq {\bf n}_*$ holds, and the correction becomes negligible, resulting in eq.~(\ref{eq:optimaloptimal_explicit}) of the main text. 
Although the above correction is not accounted for in the main text supposing small $(\partial_x\partial_y-\partial_y\partial_x)z_*$, the correction may be iteratively computed with the initialization of ${\bf n}_*^\prime \simeq {\bf n}_*$. 
The correction for $P(\Gamma|{\bf a})$ other than eq.~(\ref{eq:prior_Gamma}) is similarly evaluated using eqs.~(\ref{eq:n_exactseries}), (\ref{eq:thetaflucutaion_exact}), and (\ref{eq:phiflucutaion_exact}). 
