ing that context-enriched approaches significantly enhance performance. The dataset's creation involved both automated question generation and human verification to ensure quality. \cite{bariah2023largegenerativeaimodels} research fine-tuned multiple LLMs (BERT, RoBERTa, GPT-2) on telecom-specific language from 3GPP technical documents, achieving 84.6\% accuracy in identifying working groups, demonstrating the effectiveness of domain adaptation for telecom language understanding. The research established that even models with fewer parameters could perform effectively when properly fine-tuned on domain-specific content. \cite{dandoush2024largelanguagemodelsmeet} proposes integrating Large Language Models and multi-agent systems into network slicing management frameworks to automate intent translation, resource mapping, and lifecycle management. The authors argue that this approach can address the complexity and distributed nature of future networks, while also identifying key challenges such as scalability and interoperability that must be addressed for effective AI-driven orchestration. \cite{bornea2024telcoragnavigatingchallengesretrievalaugmented} envisions how large GenAI models can transform wireless networks into autonomous, self-evolving systems where multi-modal models trained on diverse telecom data can handle multiple downstream tasks without requiring separate specialized models. The authors specifically connect these capabilities to future 6G networks and collective intelligence paradigms.\\Almost all prior works focused on improving the performance of popular language models against a known or curated benchmarks by either curating datasets for finetuning or by enriching the context through RAG or similar mechanisms. All of these points to inherent limitations of LLMs such as memory and tokenization.

We compare recent works applying generative AI to telecom using LLMs. TSpec-LLM \cite{nikbakht2024tspecllmopensourcedatasetllm} introduced a dataset covering 3GPP documents from Release 8 to 19, significantly boosting GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 accuracy from below $51\%$ to above $71\%$ through Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). \cite{lin2024primergenerativeaitelecom} proposed a practical framework highlighting RAG’s importance for connecting LLMs to telecom-specific knowledge, notably demonstrating an O-RAN chatbot gaining industry recognition and providing open-source implementations for real-world utility. Telco-RAG \cite{bornea2024telcoragnavigatingchallengesretrievalaugmented} specialized in addressing challenges of applying RAG to technical telecom documentation, particularly complex 3GPP standards, offering guidelines relevant to broader technical domains. TeleQnA \cite{maatouk2023teleqnabenchmarkdatasetassess} introduced the first benchmark with 10,000 telecom $Q\&A$ pairs, indicating advanced LLMs struggle with complex standards and showing context-enriched approaches substantially enhance performance. The dataset was created via automated generation with human verification. \cite{bariah2023largegenerativeaimodels} fine-tuned BERT, RoBERTa, and GPT-2 on telecom language from 3GPP documents, achieving $84.6\%$ accuracy in identifying working groups, proving domain-specific adaptation is effective even for smaller models. In \cite{dandoush2024largelanguagemodelsmeet} LLMs multi-agent systems for network slicing management is introduced. Scalability and interoperability were identified to be the key challenges for effective AI-driven orchestration in future networks. In \cite{bornea2024telcoragnavigatingchallengesretrievalaugmented} the authors envisioned GenAI transforming wireless networks into autonomous systems, using multi-modal models trained on diverse telecom data.

