\opening{Dear Guest Editors of the IPM SI on Dis/Misinformation Mining from Social Media,}
%\setlength{\parindent}{0.7cm}

It is our pleasure to submit our original manuscript
\textit{`Chasing bots while trading between effectiveness and computational costs'} for possible publication to Information Processing and Management. The main objective of our research is to conduct an exploratory study on the effectiveness of general-purpose bot detectors, also when bots operate in a coordinated manner.
Using Twitter as a benchmark, we consider different features of an account, each of which has a different complexity in terms of the amount of time it takes to get the information to calculate it.

As contributions, we provide
\begin{enumerate} 
\item 
an investigation of the performances of well-established learning algorithms, trained with different sets of features, on six recently released datasets of bots and human-operated accounts;
\item 
a critical discussion of when a simple approach, based on lightweight features, is sufficient and when it is necessary to use more refined techniques;
\item 
a reconfirmation of the need to keep up with the never-ending plague of evolving bots, as advocated in past work.
  \end{enumerate} 

We would like to stress that the provided novel experimental results have not been published elsewhere,
nor currently submitted to other venues. All authors have approved the
manuscript and they agree with its submission.

Looking forward to hearing from you, we thank in advance for your
attention.

\closing{ Best regards,}
